00:06:40.688 --> 00:06:43.651
So the way we start is
if you could just say your name,

00:06:43.831 --> 00:06:46.333
how old you are,
where we are, and today's date.

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:50.496
My name is Larry Kramer.

00:06:50.757 --> 00:06:59.184
We are in my study in my lover's
in my house in New Preston, Connecticut.

00:07:02.327 --> 00:07:02.907
How old are you?

00:07:02.927 --> 00:07:03.167
I'm 68.

00:07:04.611 --> 00:07:05.171
Mazel Tov.

00:07:05.251 --> 00:07:06.352
And what's today's date?

00:07:06.372 --> 00:07:10.993
That I've lasted this long is
a major miracle.

00:07:11.894 --> 00:07:13.194
And today is November?

00:07:13.214 --> 00:07:13.254
15th.

00:07:13.214 --> 00:07:13.254
2003.

00:07:14.295 --> 00:07:19.557
Well, Larry, you know,
you've been interviewed

00:07:19.577 --> 00:07:21.637
many times and you have a lot to say.

00:07:22.438 --> 00:07:25.259
And what we really want to do are ask you
the questions that

00:07:25.799 --> 00:07:30.621
you probably have not repeated ad nauseum
before and that maybe are more

00:07:30.661 --> 00:07:34.162
of an internal conversation from people
who were inside ACT UP together.

00:07:35.623 --> 00:07:40.548
So we're not gonna be asking you
these generic questions of things

00:07:40.568 --> 00:07:42.870
that anyone
who's interested can find other places.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:45.612
I just wanna ask you
a few background questions.

00:07:46.653 --> 00:07:48.575
When did your family come to this country?

00:07:51.317 --> 00:07:53.439
Well, my father was born in this country.

00:07:54.821 --> 00:07:59.385
And his mother was from Russia.

00:08:01.366 --> 00:08:03.166
and no one knows
where his father was from.

00:08:03.186 --> 00:08:08.768
And my mother
came when she was four, also from Russia.

00:08:08.808 --> 00:08:09.948
From Russia, okay.

00:08:10.848 --> 00:08:15.869
When you were growing up, did your parents
participate in any kind

00:08:15.909 --> 00:08:18.590
of politics or community activity?

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:21.770
Yes and no.

00:08:26.351 --> 00:08:32.355
Throughout most of my, from
when I was about six till when I went away

00:08:32.375 --> 00:08:37.819
to Yale, my mother ran a suburban chapter
of the American Red Cross.

00:08:37.859 --> 00:08:42.463
She was called the home service director,
basically the second person in charge,

00:08:42.523 --> 00:08:46.906
and dealt with taking care
of all the problems,

00:08:47.647 --> 00:08:52.190
mostly having to do with the war
and servicemen and all fighting

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:53.671
and their families and things like that.

00:08:55.034 --> 00:09:04.562
My father worked for the government
as a lawyer and was, I think,

00:09:04.582 --> 00:09:12.188
probably quite puzzled
by the terrible things that were going on

00:09:12.228 --> 00:09:15.411
in the government
through the 50s around him.

00:09:15.971 --> 00:09:18.814
He had never done anything,
but he had friends who we all did.

00:09:18.874 --> 00:09:21.536
People living across
the hall from us disappeared one night.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:27.094
because the husband
had been named by McCarthy.

00:09:28.075 --> 00:09:31.257
Kids in my school
suddenly weren't there anymore,

00:09:31.357 --> 00:09:32.919
so I was surrounded by a lot of that.

00:09:32.999 --> 00:09:33.639
Where did they go?

00:09:35.681 --> 00:09:37.062
Where did they go when they disappeared?

00:09:37.082 --> 00:09:37.823
Who knew?

00:09:38.343 --> 00:09:38.664
Who knew?

00:09:41.066 --> 00:09:46.610
The kid, I don't know if there was
a famous story about a man called Service

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:53.479
who was fired by I don't even remember
who was president then, Eisenhower

00:09:53.499 --> 00:10:00.663
or somebody, for some presumed malfeasance
in the China department.

00:10:00.723 --> 00:10:02.444
He just disappeared, and the kid
disappeared,

00:10:02.464 --> 00:10:04.705
and the family disappeared with him.

00:10:04.785 --> 00:10:07.366
They surfaced again at Harvard
and Radcliffe, I might add.

00:10:08.447 --> 00:10:09.467
What was the name of the suburb?

00:10:12.887 --> 00:10:14.048
Mount Rainier, Maryland.

00:10:14.428 --> 00:10:18.410
It's just on the wrong side of,
it's in the wrong side of Washington

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:22.012
and what's Prince George's County,
which is pretty much black now.

00:10:22.032 --> 00:10:26.374
Then it was exceedingly rural.

00:10:27.034 --> 00:10:32.277
And it was the Prince George's County
chapter of the American Red Cross.

00:10:32.537 --> 00:10:36.359
And that covered an enormous area,
almost to Baltimore,

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:38.220
from Washington to Baltimore.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:47.685
And so she was always schlepping out
When I was a kid, I was taken to families

00:10:47.745 --> 00:10:51.846
whose kids had rheumatic fever,
and I was put to work on the blood mobile

00:10:51.947 --> 00:10:54.588
and all that sort of thing, which I loved.

00:10:54.608 --> 00:10:58.089
Okay, so the community involvement
was really something

00:10:58.109 --> 00:10:59.690
that your family emphasized.

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:08.193
Well, my mother was very much
a teacher and an exceedingly moral woman.

00:11:11.465 --> 00:11:16.506
that it was our duty to help, yes.

00:11:16.666 --> 00:11:21.747
And she was able to do it, and she took
a great deal of pride in her work.

00:11:22.128 --> 00:11:23.068
Drove my father nuts.

00:11:24.868 --> 00:11:28.369
So before GMHC,
had you ever been involved in

00:11:28.429 --> 00:11:30.590
any kind of political organizing?

00:11:35.271 --> 00:11:36.231
I would have to say no.

00:11:36.871 --> 00:11:37.231
Okay.

00:11:37.371 --> 00:11:39.192
Do you know why, thinking back?

00:11:39.972 --> 00:11:40.612
Why I wasn't?

00:11:40.752 --> 00:11:40.932
Yeah.

00:11:43.985 --> 00:11:46.188
I don't think
it came from a lack of interest.

00:11:46.248 --> 00:11:48.890
I think it came from a lack of ability.

00:11:48.971 --> 00:11:49.651
I grew up late.

00:11:49.972 --> 00:11:50.933
I was a late bloomer.

00:11:52.054 --> 00:11:53.515
I was a very unhappy child.

00:11:53.896 --> 00:11:56.659
I was a very unhappy teenager.

00:11:56.679 --> 00:11:58.981
I tried to kill myself at Yale.

00:12:00.183 --> 00:12:04.067
And it took a long time
to pull myself out of those troughs.

00:12:05.828 --> 00:12:07.969
I went fairly quickly.

00:12:07.989 --> 00:12:13.251
I was transferred to England to work for
Columbia Pictures there,

00:12:13.731 --> 00:12:17.973
and that was probably what saved my life
in a way, because I was

00:12:18.033 --> 00:12:22.014
able to be psychoanalyzed for
very little money,

00:12:22.434 --> 00:12:25.175
and it was enormously helpful.

00:12:25.275 --> 00:12:28.516
I wanted to write,
and I was terrified to write.

00:12:28.536 --> 00:12:32.538
I was afraid to write, as so many writers,
young writers, wannabes are.

00:12:33.997 --> 00:12:38.059
And through the analysis,
I was able to work through all of that.

00:12:39.019 --> 00:12:42.301
And the first thing I really wrote was
my screenplay for Women in Love,

00:12:42.501 --> 00:12:46.263
which turned out to give me
a certain amount of confidence.

00:12:47.583 --> 00:12:50.825
So through the 60s,
when all the political shit was going down

00:12:50.865 --> 00:12:52.226
in America, I was living in London.

00:12:53.526 --> 00:12:55.448
That screenplay
was the first thing that you wrote?

00:12:55.468 --> 00:12:55.628
Yes.

00:12:56.649 --> 00:12:59.071
Now, how did you get that gig,
if you had never written anything before?

00:12:59.091 --> 00:13:00.232
Oh, I worked in the film industry.

00:13:01.493 --> 00:13:02.854
It was the first thing I wrote on my own.

00:13:02.894 --> 00:13:05.776
I helped write a movie called
Here We Go Around the Mulberry Bush.

00:13:06.577 --> 00:13:07.538
I worked in the film industry.

00:13:07.558 --> 00:13:13.022
I was assistant to the president
of a bunch of First Columbia and then

00:13:13.082 --> 00:13:15.785
United Artists, and my job
was to find writers and find stories

00:13:15.825 --> 00:13:17.106
and find books for movies.

00:13:17.966 --> 00:13:19.707
and work with young directors and all.

00:13:19.747 --> 00:13:23.228
And London in the 60s
was the film capital of the world.

00:13:23.868 --> 00:13:28.110
Hollywood was on the down map
and Rome had been the 50s

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:30.030
and the 60s and part of the 70s.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:32.071
All the big movies came out of London.

00:13:32.791 --> 00:13:33.991
And I worked for Columbia.

00:13:35.112 --> 00:13:38.713
I worked with,
we were the king of the heap.

00:13:38.733 --> 00:13:42.134
We made movies like Suddenly Last Summer,
and Bridge on the River Kwai,

00:13:42.194 --> 00:13:44.935
and Lawrence of Arabia,
and Dr. Strangelove, and

00:13:45.155 --> 00:13:46.915
Pumpkin Eater, and I mean, go on and on.

00:13:47.695 --> 00:13:49.096
And it was just incredible.

00:13:49.116 --> 00:13:51.757
So you just said, I want to write this,
and they said, okay, give the kid a break?

00:13:51.777 --> 00:13:52.357
No, no, no, no.

00:13:52.457 --> 00:13:52.877
What happened?

00:13:56.427 --> 00:13:57.287
You're interested in all this?

00:13:57.348 --> 00:13:57.648
Okay.

00:13:57.788 --> 00:14:01.471
I mean, I'm just trying to understand
how your first assignment

00:14:01.511 --> 00:14:02.472
could be something so...

00:14:02.532 --> 00:14:08.116
Well, I wanted to write,
and I finally found out I could.

00:14:08.136 --> 00:14:09.397
I basically wanted to produce movies.

00:14:11.739 --> 00:14:14.360
And I worked in an office,
and how do you find money

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:15.900
to make movies and all these things?

00:14:16.261 --> 00:14:20.722
In those days, there was no such thing
as video, and you couldn't make the kind

00:14:20.742 --> 00:14:22.463
of movies that you could make today
for next

00:14:22.503 --> 00:14:23.903
to no money that the kids can make.

00:14:23.943 --> 00:14:25.364
You had to do it through a studio.

00:14:25.404 --> 00:14:26.404
That was the only way.

00:14:26.424 --> 00:14:29.045
You couldn't get
any kind of distribution any other way.

00:14:30.525 --> 00:14:35.387
So part of my job was finding
young directors, and I found

00:14:35.407 --> 00:14:37.608
a young director called Silvio Narzano.

00:14:38.408 --> 00:14:43.051
who went on to direct Georgie Girl,
which was a big success,

00:14:43.091 --> 00:14:44.772
which was sort of my package.

00:14:45.652 --> 00:14:53.397
And in gratitude, he said, I have a book
I would like to make as a movie,

00:14:54.138 --> 00:14:55.418
and I'll let you produce it.

00:14:55.759 --> 00:14:56.959
You can go out and get the money.

00:14:57.099 --> 00:14:58.160
And it was Women in Love.

00:14:59.181 --> 00:15:00.822
That's basically how it happened.

00:15:00.922 --> 00:15:02.563
And then it went on from there.

00:15:02.623 --> 00:15:04.044
So you made a terrible movie.

00:15:04.204 --> 00:15:06.385
It becomes involved
how we got to Ken Russell and all that.

00:15:06.445 --> 00:15:07.346
But that's basically it.

00:15:08.206 --> 00:15:08.686
Oh, I got it.

00:15:09.407 --> 00:15:14.148
So then, okay, so when you,
and this was actually Jim's question,

00:15:14.168 --> 00:15:15.469
and I think it's a really good one.

00:15:16.749 --> 00:15:22.031
Since you had not really been involved in
political activity before, and especially

00:15:22.191 --> 00:15:26.953
in the book Faggots, you have
a very critical view towards the social,

00:15:27.173 --> 00:15:29.074
the way
that the gay community was constructed.

00:15:29.094 --> 00:15:29.814
Still do.

00:15:30.594 --> 00:15:34.416
What was it that made you think
that people should be accountable

00:15:34.496 --> 00:15:35.036
and could be?

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:43.113
Well, Faggots has
a slightly different genesis

00:15:44.073 --> 00:15:47.455
than Normal Heart and that AIDS.

00:15:48.636 --> 00:15:54.179
I was very much in love with a man who
I now live with, but that was

00:15:54.039 --> 00:15:58.761
17 years earlier, and I wanted
him to fall in

00:15:58.801 --> 00:15:59.702
love with me, and he wouldn't.

00:16:00.002 --> 00:16:00.382
He didn't.

00:16:00.662 --> 00:16:02.183
Or he loved me,
but he wouldn't live with me.

00:16:03.684 --> 00:16:09.413
And those were the days of extreme,
whatever you want to call it, hedonism.

00:16:13.337 --> 00:16:18.381
I went out faggots as about a guy
coming back to America or coming into

00:16:18.401 --> 00:16:25.166
the gay world, sort of naive
and inexperienced with that world.

00:16:25.346 --> 00:16:28.008
Because there wasn't much
of a gay world in those days, remember.

00:16:28.028 --> 00:16:29.549
It wasn't a community.

00:16:29.589 --> 00:16:30.850
It wasn't a political community.

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:32.952
It was very little of all that.

00:16:33.752 --> 00:16:37.315
It was exceedingly diffuse, dispersed.

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:41.161
And I went out to look at that world.

00:16:41.181 --> 00:16:42.783
It was my shrink's idea.

00:16:42.803 --> 00:16:48.387
Why don't you go out and explore what
the gay world is really like?

00:16:48.427 --> 00:16:49.307
And that's what I found.

00:16:51.309 --> 00:16:54.992
Most of the stuff, in one way
or another, happened to me.

00:16:55.932 --> 00:16:58.434
And there are people
that are based on real people.

00:16:59.935 --> 00:17:01.276
Not much made up, in fact.

00:17:03.578 --> 00:17:05.960
That's not to say that one doesn't take
a certain amount of license.

00:17:09.501 --> 00:17:14.465
And I wanted to know why our love affair
didn't work out, and I came to

00:17:14.505 --> 00:17:20.710
this realization that there was,
that no love affair, no relationship

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:23.872
could withstand the baggage that
we were putting on it by having

00:17:23.932 --> 00:17:26.374
so much sex with so many different people.

00:17:27.435 --> 00:17:30.618
Who can exist through that
in a relationship

00:17:30.678 --> 00:17:33.500
without one person or both going nuts?

00:17:36.385 --> 00:17:40.408
So how could you expect that that group
of people, as you just defined them,

00:17:41.028 --> 00:17:44.110
would be
able to meet the challenge of AIDS?

00:17:45.951 --> 00:17:48.253
Sarah, that's a great, wonderful question.

00:17:49.234 --> 00:17:49.694
But I did.

00:17:49.714 --> 00:17:51.815
And I still do, and they still haven't.

00:17:54.977 --> 00:17:55.738
I guess I felt...

00:17:59.238 --> 00:18:00.059
It was a little different.

00:18:00.079 --> 00:18:01.621
We were dying.

00:18:02.782 --> 00:18:06.886
And it happened first, so far
as I could tell, to my people

00:18:06.926 --> 00:18:08.428
around me, my friends, our group.

00:18:13.959 --> 00:18:17.722
You just suddenly think, oh my God,
we've got to save our lives, I guess.

00:18:19.183 --> 00:18:20.484
To this day, I don't understand why.

00:18:21.264 --> 00:18:26.608
At its heyday, at its peak, hacked up,
how many do we have across the country?

00:18:26.388 --> 00:18:27.829
10,000 people maybe?

00:18:28.569 --> 00:18:31.011
With how many millions
of gay people in this country?

00:18:32.812 --> 00:18:33.653
How did I expect it?

00:18:33.693 --> 00:18:34.013
I didn't.

00:18:35.476 --> 00:18:40.419
This is kind of a tough question,
but if you had never been involved in

00:18:40.499 --> 00:18:44.321
fighting for anybody else,
where did you get the expectation

00:18:44.361 --> 00:18:47.183
that other people should come
and make a stand for us?

00:18:53.709 --> 00:18:55.250
I guess I never thought of it that way.

00:18:56.211 --> 00:18:58.152
We needed help
and you had to scream for it

00:18:58.232 --> 00:19:01.214
and I asked for it nicely originally.

00:19:02.215 --> 00:19:06.958
We tried to be very nice to
the New York Times and to Ed Koch

00:19:08.019 --> 00:19:13.623
and you learn very fast that you're
a faggot and it doesn't make

00:19:13.663 --> 00:19:17.046
any difference that you went to Yale
and were assistant to the presidents

00:19:17.106 --> 00:19:18.927
of a couple film companies
and that you had money.

00:19:19.848 --> 00:19:22.749
You suddenly know what
it's like to feel Like a faggot

00:19:22.769 --> 00:19:24.050
or a nigger or a kike, I did.

00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:25.731
And I have said that, and I did.

00:19:25.771 --> 00:19:27.732
And I remember the day it happened.

00:19:28.912 --> 00:19:30.093
And I didn't like it, so...

00:19:30.113 --> 00:19:33.974
I don't know if your question is...

00:19:36.315 --> 00:19:37.456
I was always an angry kid.

00:19:39.477 --> 00:19:41.558
My father and I fought like tigers.

00:19:42.258 --> 00:19:43.619
Screamed at each other all the time.

00:19:43.959 --> 00:19:47.700
I may have been unhappy
and I may have been shy, which I still am,

00:19:48.281 --> 00:19:49.721
but I knew how to yell back at him.

00:19:50.782 --> 00:19:58.261
And... I guess the one thing that
various analyses and working in AIDS

00:19:58.841 --> 00:20:02.863
or whatever we were working in taught me
was how to make the anger

00:20:02.963 --> 00:20:07.846
productive and creative and useful.

00:20:08.746 --> 00:20:11.268
But I always had the anger,
so I think it was more that.

00:20:11.328 --> 00:20:19.692
I don't know if I was I grew up
with a mother who was always dealing with

00:20:19.732 --> 00:20:21.794
what was wrong with the system.

00:20:21.834 --> 00:20:23.255
Why aren't
these people being taken care of?

00:20:23.275 --> 00:20:25.096
Blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:20:25.157 --> 00:20:27.298
She knew how to do it,
to go ask the system.

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:33.421
find money or something to get
somebody transferred, or money

00:20:33.461 --> 00:20:38.943
for sick kids
in a house, or a burned-out family.

00:20:38.983 --> 00:20:41.083
She knew how to do all that,
and she was confronting

00:20:41.123 --> 00:20:42.023
the system all the time.

00:20:42.083 --> 00:20:45.845
So I would, I guess, I don't think
that I was paying that much attention

00:20:45.885 --> 00:20:47.065
to it, but perhaps I was.

00:20:47.985 --> 00:20:51.006
Well, when did you first become
aware of direct action?

00:20:55.313 --> 00:20:57.354
I think
we all made it up as we went along.

00:20:57.374 --> 00:20:59.235
I don't know that we became aware of it.

00:21:00.736 --> 00:21:03.197
There are all these terminologies
for these things now.

00:21:05.839 --> 00:21:07.460
When did you first see people doing it?

00:21:12.322 --> 00:21:15.604
The speech I made that night
in the center, I referred to this group

00:21:15.644 --> 00:21:20.287
of Catholics that went up to Albany, 10
or 20,000 of them,

00:21:20.587 --> 00:21:23.248
and screaming at the legislature there.

00:21:23.428 --> 00:21:24.789
I said, why can't we do that?

00:21:25.854 --> 00:21:29.080
I remember pictures
of the Vietnam thing and all that.

00:21:29.882 --> 00:21:42.866
I don't deal in the... in the linguistics
of all this very well, either sexuality

00:21:42.966 --> 00:21:45.868
or homosexuality or politics.

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:50.271
I was not a political person.

00:21:50.311 --> 00:21:55.014
In London, the film world was filled with
Marxists when I worked there.

00:21:55.434 --> 00:21:58.356
A lot of my friends were directors.

00:21:59.477 --> 00:22:03.199
who all grew up with Marxist viewpoints.

00:22:03.560 --> 00:22:06.201
And, you know, I didn't know
what the fuck they were talking about.

00:22:06.982 --> 00:22:08.483
But you did know that you felt that...

00:22:08.503 --> 00:22:10.744
Tony and I joke about it a lot.

00:22:10.924 --> 00:22:15.087
You, and correct me if I'm wrong,
but the history, as I understand it,

00:22:15.147 --> 00:22:17.628
is that you felt
that GMHC was ineffective.

00:22:18.089 --> 00:22:20.370
You wanted to create
another kind of response.

00:22:21.331 --> 00:22:24.513
So you must have had
an idea of another way of acting.

00:22:25.233 --> 00:22:26.694
And I'm wondering where that came from.

00:22:32.819 --> 00:22:38.303
GMHC was
an extraordinarily moving experience.

00:22:39.985 --> 00:22:43.667
I've become very friendly
with Ana Oliveira, who's the head

00:22:43.707 --> 00:22:45.429
of it now,
and we've become very good friends.

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:50.793
We had lunch the other day
and we were talking about various problems

00:22:50.833 --> 00:22:53.775
that she's still confronting
and things that happen.

00:22:54.015 --> 00:22:56.497
I've got to tell you, I started to cry
a couple of times during lunch.

00:23:00.689 --> 00:23:07.673
Yet, I was locked in this vice there,
because I was surrounded with a board

00:23:09.034 --> 00:23:12.576
of my friends that I had put on
that board, because my brother's law firm,

00:23:12.817 --> 00:23:18.600
who still represents GMHC, drew up
this thing that we had for

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:20.341
tax exempt, and we had to have a board.

00:23:20.661 --> 00:23:25.604
I turned to the guys I danced with
and put them on the board, including

00:23:26.385 --> 00:23:28.946
Paul Popham, who was a close friend.

00:23:32.091 --> 00:23:34.153
And they didn't like anything
of what I was doing.

00:23:34.293 --> 00:23:36.435
When I would raise my voice,
when I would criticize

00:23:36.475 --> 00:23:39.939
the mayor, very fast I did all that.

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:41.601
They didn't like it.

00:23:41.741 --> 00:23:44.283
So it was hard.

00:23:44.584 --> 00:23:45.104
It was hard.

00:23:45.424 --> 00:23:46.345
And I couldn't push them.

00:23:46.746 --> 00:23:47.546
I couldn't push them.

00:23:48.267 --> 00:23:51.190
I couldn't push them.

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:54.133
And I thought at one point I could.

00:23:57.048 --> 00:24:00.975
I didn't even think when I had this, I
either go to that meeting

00:24:01.035 --> 00:24:02.437
with the mayor or else I quit.

00:24:03.038 --> 00:24:03.559
That's just Larry.

00:24:06.534 --> 00:24:10.495
Larry's MO didn't think twice
about it didn't think it in advance

00:24:10.535 --> 00:24:11.856
I'm gonna do this it just did

00:24:12.796 --> 00:24:17.718
and then I couldn't believe it
when they said go so then began

00:24:19.478 --> 00:24:22.659
these years in the wilderness from that
was like 83 84

00:24:22.719 --> 00:24:24.440
before 87 I wrote normal heart and but I

00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:32.463
Everybody was dying.

00:24:32.483 --> 00:24:34.986
I mean, how could you not respond to
that in some sort of way?

00:24:35.386 --> 00:24:41.854
Well, a lot of people didn't,
but I just couldn't believe

00:24:41.914 --> 00:24:47.189
that that this country was so hateful.

00:24:47.429 --> 00:24:50.553
So then you didn't come to
the ACT UP idea with...

00:24:50.573 --> 00:24:53.116
Preconceived notions, no.

00:24:53.276 --> 00:24:57.541
I didn't come to GMHC
with any preconceived notions either.

00:24:57.901 --> 00:25:00.124
Direct action
was ACT UP's most effective tactic.

00:25:00.144 --> 00:25:01.065
Where did that come in?

00:25:03.267 --> 00:25:09.310
The big difference between Okay,
the big difference

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:11.811
between the two organizations was this.

00:25:13.031 --> 00:25:16.732
I was determined
that whatever got started,

00:25:17.072 --> 00:25:23.673
it would not be structured,
because that's what GMHC had become.

00:25:24.173 --> 00:25:28.514
Everything had to have a job description
and approval by this and approval

00:25:28.554 --> 00:25:32.975
by that, and I didn't want that.

00:25:35.390 --> 00:25:37.312
So we went the other way.

00:25:37.372 --> 00:25:40.514
I've often described it
as democratic to a fault.

00:25:42.256 --> 00:25:45.198
And when people came and said,
we want to start this

00:25:45.238 --> 00:25:47.340
or we want to start that, fine.

00:25:47.761 --> 00:25:51.184
Because I remember GMHC
when I tried to get different groups

00:25:51.244 --> 00:25:54.927
started, different people who came
and said, I can do epidemiology,

00:25:54.967 --> 00:25:59.110
I can do—it was forever until we could get
a lawyer in there and do legal services.

00:26:00.171 --> 00:26:05.676
People came to ACT UP and said,
we want to start—we were allowed to do it.

00:26:05.716 --> 00:26:07.377
That was great.

00:26:07.878 --> 00:26:11.461
There could have been more committees,
as far as I was concerned, as long

00:26:11.481 --> 00:26:13.042
as they were focused on
this sort of thing.

00:26:14.243 --> 00:26:15.424
I tried to steer it.

00:26:15.504 --> 00:26:19.528
After a while, I didn't have any power
to do that, but you can influence things

00:26:19.708 --> 00:26:20.328
and welcome things.

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:25.975
One thing I learned, the analogy
is really with the movie business.

00:26:26.375 --> 00:26:30.000
I was trained in the movie business,
and you call it direct action,

00:26:30.020 --> 00:26:31.202
I call it putting on a show.

00:26:35.385 --> 00:26:39.988
One of the many things I learned in
the movie business is you get

00:26:40.048 --> 00:26:44.630
so much more energy out of people
if you love them, if you thank them,

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:46.371
if you say, job well done.

00:26:47.572 --> 00:26:51.654
I worked for a shit, my first boss
in the movie, a man called Mike Rackerts,

00:26:51.694 --> 00:26:55.516
who wouldn't say thank you
if... if his life depended on it.

00:26:55.596 --> 00:26:58.957
And I realized
how many people resent all that.

00:26:59.137 --> 00:27:02.078
And I worked for film directors,
Ken Russell being one of them,

00:27:02.138 --> 00:27:08.761
who would take his riding crop
and hit a camera person

00:27:08.821 --> 00:27:10.922
or someone who was in the way.

00:27:12.162 --> 00:27:12.882
And they hated him.

00:27:13.403 --> 00:27:19.785
Why Women in Love is so good is
because I saw to it that that crew

00:27:19.845 --> 00:27:23.686
was just nurtured
and loved every minute we possibly could.

00:27:23.746 --> 00:27:26.027
Give them parties,
give them thank yous, whatever.

00:27:26.748 --> 00:27:29.289
Remember their birthdays, take out
their wives, because Ken Russell

00:27:29.329 --> 00:27:30.289
was such a shit to them.

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:35.611
And the movie is all there
on the screen because of a lot of that.

00:27:37.569 --> 00:27:42.333
And I tried to do that at GMHC
in the beginning, but then when I came up

00:27:42.393 --> 00:27:45.896
against my first job description,
what is a job description?

00:27:45.996 --> 00:27:47.617
I didn't know
what the fuck a job description was.

00:27:47.677 --> 00:27:49.018
Why do we have to have a job description?

00:27:49.058 --> 00:27:50.359
Why can't we go get the best person?

00:27:50.419 --> 00:27:54.002
And then became all this business about,
you know, you gotta have this person

00:27:54.042 --> 00:27:59.465
and that person, and it's gotta be,
What do you mean,

00:27:59.485 --> 00:28:01.766
this person and that person?

00:28:01.786 --> 00:28:05.187
Well, you had to take people
whether they were good or not,

00:28:05.247 --> 00:28:09.809
because they represented
certain genders or certain colors.

00:28:09.949 --> 00:28:11.870
We didn't have
many women in the beginning.

00:28:12.230 --> 00:28:17.732
The first paid employee
of GMHC was a black person, Phil Patrick.

00:28:19.112 --> 00:28:28.520
We couldn't get black people there,
so somehow to have to have to deal with

00:28:29.441 --> 00:28:30.062
all of that.

00:28:30.602 --> 00:28:33.205
I always say, get the best person somehow,
and you get into

00:28:33.225 --> 00:28:34.527
a lot of trouble with stuff like that.

00:28:35.448 --> 00:28:36.229
No offense meant.

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:38.271
It's hard.

00:28:38.371 --> 00:28:40.554
As I said,
we were making it up as we went along.

00:28:40.574 --> 00:28:41.515
There were no rules.

00:28:42.316 --> 00:28:46.481
There were no rules with GMHC
until we got all these job descriptions.

00:28:47.722 --> 00:28:51.584
hey, it became the Red Cross,
and I'd had my Red Cross in my lifetime.

00:28:52.644 --> 00:28:56.145
And I just, they wouldn't go after
anybody, they wouldn't criticize anybody,

00:28:56.205 --> 00:28:58.446
they wouldn't picket,
they wouldn't protest,

00:28:58.646 --> 00:28:59.387
any of those things.

00:29:00.047 --> 00:29:02.068
But see, that's what I'm saying,
like picket, okay,

00:29:02.088 --> 00:29:03.028
you wanted them to picket.

00:29:03.508 --> 00:29:06.769
I mean, that's a direct action,
that's not like a bureaucratic service.

00:29:06.809 --> 00:29:09.350
Well, I didn't know, it's trying to make
yourself heard, I guess.

00:29:09.551 --> 00:29:10.611
Okay.

00:29:10.751 --> 00:29:12.472
You call it direct action and act up.

00:29:12.652 --> 00:29:15.633
Did you call it direct action
before you knew what direct action was?

00:29:17.168 --> 00:29:20.710
Well, I mean, it's, you know,
there's a long history of that kind

00:29:20.750 --> 00:29:23.432
of activity
for people trying to change the country.

00:29:23.492 --> 00:29:24.993
Well, I didn't come from that at all.

00:29:25.233 --> 00:29:26.014
I really didn't.

00:29:26.614 --> 00:29:28.155
And I guess that surprises people.

00:29:28.616 --> 00:29:31.538
So people just, so you just started ACT UP
and then people brought that with them.

00:29:34.319 --> 00:29:37.121
I remember those meetings
where we would all sit around and talk.

00:29:37.181 --> 00:29:42.045
The first action, which was, I don't,
I don't remember how it got to be

00:29:42.085 --> 00:29:47.968
Wall Street, but We were in the room.

00:29:47.988 --> 00:29:48.569
Were you there then?

00:29:48.649 --> 00:29:49.109
I don't remember.

00:29:49.129 --> 00:29:50.010
No, not the first meetings.

00:29:50.631 --> 00:29:53.033
We were in the room,
and all right, what are we going to do?

00:29:53.093 --> 00:29:59.559
We knew we had to do something public
because, because, because, I don't know,

00:29:59.599 --> 00:30:01.901
because of the Catholics that marched on
Albany, I guess,

00:30:02.541 --> 00:30:03.862
because there wasn't anything else to do.

00:30:03.943 --> 00:30:04.983
How do you get attention?

00:30:05.444 --> 00:30:10.989
And somebody said,
let's pick it, let's go against it.

00:30:12.486 --> 00:30:14.387
Let's go against the FDA,
because they were

00:30:14.428 --> 00:30:15.728
so slow in approving things.

00:30:16.189 --> 00:30:20.332
There was a big to-do over
something called—I know that was

00:30:20.352 --> 00:30:29.298
too early, but I had—Matilde said to me,
Dr. Crim, the big heavy

00:30:29.338 --> 00:30:32.781
in all of this is Frank Young at the FDA.

00:30:36.049 --> 00:30:39.131
So I sat down and I wrote an op-ed piece
for the Times, and they took it.

00:30:40.532 --> 00:30:44.975
It's called
the FDA's Callous Response to AIDS.

00:30:44.995 --> 00:30:45.696
Was it called AIDS?

00:30:45.856 --> 00:30:46.056
No.

00:30:46.256 --> 00:30:48.418
Yes, it was called to AIDS.

00:30:49.827 --> 00:30:55.628
They took it, and it ran on the very day
that we had the demo, so we were

00:30:55.728 --> 00:31:00.509
able to pass out
at Wall Street these flyers.

00:31:00.849 --> 00:31:07.131
I had got Joe Papp to make an effigy
in the shop of the public of Frank Young,

00:31:07.171 --> 00:31:10.911
and we hung him
an effigy out down there on Wall Street.

00:31:15.793 --> 00:31:16.533
Where did it come from?

00:31:16.614 --> 00:31:19.775
I think it just came from all of us
talking with each other all the time.

00:31:19.835 --> 00:31:20.255
I don't know.

00:31:20.756 --> 00:31:22.356
How did you get your piece in the Times?

00:31:22.396 --> 00:31:23.257
Did you know people there?

00:31:30.280 --> 00:31:31.281
Not like I know them now.

00:31:31.301 --> 00:31:40.288
I have to say, I don't know.

00:31:40.308 --> 00:31:41.550
I don't remember.

00:31:41.651 --> 00:31:42.693
Well, let's get to ACT UP.

00:31:43.194 --> 00:31:45.959
What were some of your concrete,
some of the concrete projects

00:31:45.979 --> 00:31:47.161
that you worked on in ACT UP?

00:31:48.553 --> 00:31:53.854
I like to think I was, I don't think
anybody did anything

00:31:53.974 --> 00:31:55.475
specifically all by themselves.

00:31:55.575 --> 00:31:59.396
I think that was the great thing
about it is that we worked as a group.

00:31:59.456 --> 00:32:03.677
I tried to be like a den mother,
I think, more than anything.

00:32:04.717 --> 00:32:09.958
If people started getting, there were
big disagreements between a lot of people

00:32:09.998 --> 00:32:11.298
and I would try to smooth them down.

00:32:11.318 --> 00:32:13.439
I don't know if you remember
the whole thing with Suzanne Phillips.

00:32:14.399 --> 00:32:14.939
What was that?

00:32:15.059 --> 00:32:16.320
I don't know.

00:32:16.360 --> 00:32:18.061
She had a big fight with some other woman.

00:32:20.102 --> 00:32:21.422
I've got it written down somewhere.

00:32:23.143 --> 00:32:27.345
There were a lot of personality things.

00:32:29.986 --> 00:32:31.727
If somebody had a great idea,
I'd just let

00:32:31.747 --> 00:32:33.228
them know it was a great idea.

00:32:33.748 --> 00:32:36.489
If I didn't like the idea,
I would try to say maybe we can work it

00:32:36.550 --> 00:32:37.490
into a good idea, whatever.

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:40.131
It was a lot of behind-the-scenes
stuff, I think, more than anything.

00:32:42.994 --> 00:32:47.079
trying to encourage people to remember in
the beginning nobody would work go on

00:32:47.099 --> 00:32:49.362
a committee particularly
and trying to encourage people

00:32:49.482 --> 00:32:52.907
to to join committees because committees
where I thought were so important

00:32:53.688 --> 00:32:56.452
and that that was how the work
was going to get done and I used to go to

00:32:56.472 --> 00:32:59.775
all the committees and Which committees
did you like to be part of?

00:32:59.795 --> 00:33:02.197
Well, I became very attached,
as you know,

00:33:02.217 --> 00:33:03.438
to treatment and data, mostly.

00:33:04.138 --> 00:33:07.421
Iris Long was this incredible thing
that happened to ACT UP, just incredible.

00:33:07.441 --> 00:33:13.386
You want to talk about the miracle
of grassroots organizing, This woman

00:33:13.586 --> 00:33:15.167
just shows up one day,
this straight woman,

00:33:15.187 --> 00:33:18.530
and after a few meetings, she gets up
and she says, you guys don't know shit,

00:33:19.531 --> 00:33:21.733
in her nice way, and I'll teach you.

00:33:22.534 --> 00:33:25.316
And she did, and she took two,
three people initially,

00:33:25.336 --> 00:33:27.518
and then there were four or five,
and then there were bigger,

00:33:27.578 --> 00:33:29.960
and she taught them everything they knew,
and then they branched out and met

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:35.744
other people, Boston
and at the NIH and all of that.

00:33:36.865 --> 00:33:47.351
By the time, a few years later,
I would take Mark or to a meeting,

00:33:47.431 --> 00:33:49.372
and they would think he was a doctor.

00:33:49.712 --> 00:33:52.695
That was the amazing thing,
this incredible meeting where we were at

00:33:52.735 --> 00:33:58.119
Bristol-Myers in some big office building,
and we were at a break in the John,

00:33:58.199 --> 00:34:03.663
and the schmuck from Bristol-Myers,
we were pissing next to each other

00:34:03.723 --> 00:34:08.587
in the men's room, and he looks over at me
and he says, that Harrington,

00:34:08.767 --> 00:34:10.449
it is Dr. Harrington, isn't it?

00:34:11.069 --> 00:34:14.732
And, you know, I just felt
he's one of my children, whatever, so...

00:34:15.279 --> 00:34:17.362
So when you say you brought him
to a meeting, did you make

00:34:17.382 --> 00:34:18.904
the contact with Crystal Myers?

00:34:19.584 --> 00:34:20.205
Crystal Myers?

00:34:26.233 --> 00:34:27.314
Who made what contact?

00:34:27.374 --> 00:34:31.319
I don't know that anybody can remember
who made what contacts, basically.

00:34:35.700 --> 00:34:39.602
There were people working on
specific drugs who, by that time,

00:34:39.642 --> 00:34:42.724
who were dealing with representatives
in the drug company

00:34:42.764 --> 00:34:45.765
and able to ask for specific meetings.

00:34:47.767 --> 00:34:49.968
I was trotted out
to scare people by that time.

00:34:50.628 --> 00:34:54.990
I remember another meeting at
Hoffman LaRoche, I remember a lot

00:34:55.010 --> 00:34:59.513
of meetings at Hoffman LaRoche,
where the man next to me was shaking.

00:35:01.141 --> 00:35:04.964
And I turned to the guy on the other side
who also, I said, what's wrong with him?

00:35:04.984 --> 00:35:06.646
He said, he's scared of you.

00:35:06.666 --> 00:35:08.987
And I thought, hey, wow.

00:35:10.008 --> 00:35:11.349
Why do you think they were scared of you?

00:35:13.871 --> 00:35:16.213
Because by then I'd had all
of this publicity

00:35:16.754 --> 00:35:18.335
as the angriest gay man in the world.

00:35:18.355 --> 00:35:19.476
Are you familiar with all that?

00:35:21.198 --> 00:35:22.498
And fine.

00:35:23.499 --> 00:35:24.679
I think I'm a pussycat.

00:35:25.919 --> 00:35:28.620
And people would always say to me
after the interview, you're so soft-spoken

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:30.621
and so nice,
how come you've got this reputation?

00:35:31.361 --> 00:35:31.721
Fine.

00:35:32.001 --> 00:35:33.122
I'm in the movie business.

00:35:33.182 --> 00:35:38.363
I know that that publicity of that image
was useful to act up and useful to me.

00:35:38.423 --> 00:35:39.804
We could play good cop, bad cop.

00:35:40.844 --> 00:35:42.224
And let them be scared of me.

00:35:42.264 --> 00:35:42.484
Fine.

00:35:42.504 --> 00:35:43.445
So you were the bad cop?

00:35:44.025 --> 00:35:44.745
Whatever they wanted.

00:35:44.785 --> 00:35:45.665
Who was the good cop?

00:35:46.806 --> 00:35:47.786
Well, why...

00:35:50.342 --> 00:35:57.128
The good cop were, the bad cops
were all the kids on the floor.

00:35:57.269 --> 00:36:01.793
And the good cops were all the people
inside doing the negotiating.

00:36:01.913 --> 00:36:04.155
That's what it boiled, in the end
that's what,

00:36:04.175 --> 00:36:05.536
and that I learned in the movie business.

00:36:06.377 --> 00:36:10.919
That's how every successful company
of any kind really works, is you got

00:36:10.939 --> 00:36:16.402
a shit executive who fires everybody
or cuts everybody's budget, and you got

00:36:16.422 --> 00:36:22.765
a saint executive who keeps everybody
happy and don't think they don't talk to

00:36:22.825 --> 00:36:25.127
each other
at the end of the day to compare notes.

00:36:25.267 --> 00:36:29.689
And that's basically what I think
I was trying to steer ACT UP toward.

00:36:30.069 --> 00:36:31.350
Towards a dual strategy.

00:36:31.431 --> 00:36:31.731
Yes.

00:36:31.931 --> 00:36:40.079
I mean, there was a lot of flack
from people like Maxine about going inside

00:36:40.179 --> 00:36:41.881
when we were finally able to go inside.

00:36:41.941 --> 00:36:42.942
And I said, are you crazy?

00:36:42.962 --> 00:36:44.544
Of course you go inside.

00:36:44.564 --> 00:36:47.126
They let you inside.

00:36:47.607 --> 00:36:48.688
What can you do on the outside?

00:36:48.708 --> 00:36:49.969
You can only go so far on the outside.

00:36:51.901 --> 00:36:58.523
Anyway, I'm convinced that the destruction
of ACT UP

00:36:58.583 --> 00:37:04.544
was the severing of this dual nature.

00:37:04.685 --> 00:37:08.305
What destroyed ACT UP is when treatment
and data picked up their marbles

00:37:08.346 --> 00:37:12.327
and went somewhere else,
leaving only the bad guys, so to speak.

00:37:13.167 --> 00:37:14.167
Why do you think that happened?

00:37:19.699 --> 00:37:22.381
drunk on hubris, drunk on their brains,
drunk on the fact

00:37:22.401 --> 00:37:24.782
that they were—the very things
Maxine predicted,

00:37:24.842 --> 00:37:30.585
I might add—that they were
drunk on their power.

00:37:30.966 --> 00:37:34.227
They could sit down with the head of
Bristol-Myers or the chief scientist,

00:37:34.287 --> 00:37:38.310
or they could call all these people up,
and they could do it

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:41.151
on their own from then on.

00:37:42.012 --> 00:37:45.074
They didn't need anyone
fighting on the outside for them.

00:37:45.114 --> 00:37:47.195
Perhaps they even became
a little ashamed of us, I don't know.

00:37:49.264 --> 00:37:50.885
But I will never forgive them for it.

00:37:52.186 --> 00:37:53.347
I feel that strongly about it.

00:37:54.187 --> 00:37:57.970
To this day, Mark
and I don't talk, haven't done for years.

00:37:59.951 --> 00:38:04.114
But hadn't you yourself pretty much
separated from ACT UP before that?

00:38:04.154 --> 00:38:04.994
I never separated.

00:38:05.054 --> 00:38:06.876
I don't know where that came from.

00:38:06.896 --> 00:38:09.017
Well, you stopped coming to
meetings at a certain point.

00:38:09.277 --> 00:38:11.879
Only because I was writing
a play or I was sick or whatever.

00:38:12.799 --> 00:38:13.500
I did do a play.

00:38:19.234 --> 00:38:23.496
We had the longest run, I think,
of any grassroots organization

00:38:24.556 --> 00:38:27.317
ever of success.

00:38:27.738 --> 00:38:30.458
You have to pull back every once
in a while just

00:38:30.519 --> 00:38:31.619
because you get burned out.

00:38:32.399 --> 00:38:33.099
You get tired.

00:38:33.119 --> 00:38:36.501
You couldn't disassociate yourself
from MacDuff.

00:38:36.521 --> 00:38:39.642
It isn't like GMHC where they throw you
off the board or whatever.

00:38:40.342 --> 00:38:44.251
Mind you, we wish we sometimes had
a mechanism for getting rid of people.

00:38:44.291 --> 00:38:46.516
We had a number of people
who we should have gotten rid of

00:38:47.399 --> 00:38:49.624
who stole money from us
and all that, but we didn't.

00:38:51.052 --> 00:38:55.175
Larry, this may be a bad memory,
but I seem to remember one time you quit

00:38:55.255 --> 00:38:58.336
because you wanted
ACT UP to have a president.

00:38:58.917 --> 00:38:59.637
Did I make that up?

00:39:00.378 --> 00:39:00.758
I think so.

00:39:00.878 --> 00:39:02.759
You didn't bring in
a proposal about structuring?

00:39:02.799 --> 00:39:03.560
A president, no.

00:39:03.640 --> 00:39:05.421
Okay, sorry, excuse me for that then.

00:39:05.941 --> 00:39:07.222
Oh, okay.

00:39:07.542 --> 00:39:08.823
A president?

00:39:11.264 --> 00:39:13.546
I remember you got angry
because you wanted

00:39:13.586 --> 00:39:14.727
a certain kind of structure.

00:39:14.747 --> 00:39:17.568
We can talk about that in a minute.

00:39:17.588 --> 00:39:18.669
Okay, let's put in a new tape.

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:29.486
And to have to deal with that size
of a room got to be distressing

00:39:29.926 --> 00:39:33.289
and boring for a lot of people too.

00:39:35.691 --> 00:39:39.434
Because of the rotating nature
of the chairs,

00:39:45.768 --> 00:39:49.472
People were constantly looking for
ways to make it all go smoother.

00:39:49.492 --> 00:39:51.614
And we used to have meetings about that.

00:39:51.674 --> 00:39:52.855
How can we make it go smoother?

00:39:52.875 --> 00:39:55.117
How can we get rid of the bullshit?

00:39:55.157 --> 00:39:56.518
How can we cut to the chase?

00:39:56.578 --> 00:39:56.919
Whatever.

00:39:58.723 --> 00:39:59.784
And there were a lot of ideas.

00:39:59.844 --> 00:40:01.405
I don't think
a general was ever one of them.

00:40:01.485 --> 00:40:06.908
I think at some point I got,
as things really got awful, I became

00:40:06.968 --> 00:40:14.172
much more militant in thinking of,
in trying to, in visualizing,

00:40:15.233 --> 00:40:19.215
act up as an army,
which didn't go down very well.

00:40:19.615 --> 00:40:24.758
And I think at one point
I actually tried to interest people

00:40:24.938 --> 00:40:29.783
in taking gun shooting practice.

00:40:30.243 --> 00:40:31.304
Yes, I heard that speech.

00:40:31.404 --> 00:40:34.208
You invoked the ear gun, actually.

00:40:34.228 --> 00:40:37.371
I'll invoke anybody if it's useful.

00:40:37.771 --> 00:40:38.492
But I believed it.

00:40:39.413 --> 00:40:40.475
Tell me, let's talk about that.

00:40:40.555 --> 00:40:41.315
Let's talk about that.

00:40:41.496 --> 00:40:43.097
What was on your mind at that time?

00:40:46.521 --> 00:40:47.943
Oh, who knows what was on my mind?

00:40:51.781 --> 00:40:53.923
You have to be able to go back
to those times

00:40:54.023 --> 00:40:56.004
and realize how totally everything was.

00:40:57.506 --> 00:41:00.528
People would come up to, sick people,
guys would come up to me at meetings

00:41:04.371 --> 00:41:07.453
and would beg me, isn't there anything?

00:41:07.954 --> 00:41:10.255
Isn't anything out there for me?

00:41:10.516 --> 00:41:11.236
Tell me what you know.

00:41:11.256 --> 00:41:11.857
Who do I call?

00:41:11.897 --> 00:41:12.637
You know everybody.

00:41:13.418 --> 00:41:13.958
Blah, blah, blah.

00:41:13.998 --> 00:41:14.579
What's coming?

00:41:14.959 --> 00:41:15.660
Can I hold on?

00:41:16.160 --> 00:41:16.700
All the time.

00:41:18.956 --> 00:41:19.496
That's hard.

00:41:20.457 --> 00:41:20.958
That's hard.

00:41:25.041 --> 00:41:27.162
And we still, even
though we might have been inside

00:41:27.242 --> 00:41:31.566
certain meetings,
we still really weren't inside.

00:41:31.626 --> 00:41:33.247
This was in the days
when we weren't in there yet

00:41:33.347 --> 00:41:34.408
and we didn't have the drugs yet.

00:41:38.615 --> 00:41:41.617
The best demo
we ever had probably was at NIH.

00:41:42.517 --> 00:41:43.238
No publicity.

00:41:43.978 --> 00:41:44.759
Nobody picked it up.

00:41:44.839 --> 00:41:46.580
There was a fire in Washington that day.

00:41:47.440 --> 00:41:49.222
All the papers went to cover the fire.

00:41:49.622 --> 00:41:52.604
That incredible show for nothing.

00:41:54.525 --> 00:41:56.666
And it's like, as you as a playwright
will know, it's like

00:41:56.726 --> 00:41:58.227
closing on opening night somehow.

00:41:58.587 --> 00:41:59.788
All your work goes for naught.

00:42:00.909 --> 00:42:03.650
So you get, you increase
the desperation quotient

00:42:03.710 --> 00:42:05.211
inside yourself, I guess.

00:42:07.529 --> 00:42:09.630
Did you take gun training?

00:42:10.210 --> 00:42:13.471
No, but I would have.

00:42:13.571 --> 00:42:27.195
My best friend is Roger McFarland,
who had been a Navy SEAL,

00:42:28.595 --> 00:42:29.755
the equivalent of a Green Beret.

00:42:30.335 --> 00:42:32.936
He'd had training in a lot of that stuff.

00:42:34.256 --> 00:42:39.923
We used to fantasize about if the two
of us could somehow just go

00:42:40.003 --> 00:42:44.046
and knock off people around the country.

00:42:44.446 --> 00:42:45.587
Whoever, Jesse Helms.

00:42:46.828 --> 00:42:48.209
People, enemies, basically.

00:42:48.630 --> 00:42:52.092
Why do you think that nobody who died of
AIDS took anyone

00:42:52.132 --> 00:42:53.353
with them from the other side?

00:42:54.374 --> 00:42:55.015
Good question.

00:42:56.376 --> 00:42:56.976
Good question.

00:42:58.678 --> 00:43:04.262
In this book I'm working on
now, it deals a lot with...

00:43:06.670 --> 00:43:07.550
The American people?

00:43:07.830 --> 00:43:08.030
Yes.

00:43:09.111 --> 00:43:13.152
And it deals a lot
with this whole question with the Jews

00:43:13.892 --> 00:43:17.013
and with gays not fighting for
their rights all through the years.

00:43:17.413 --> 00:43:21.694
Almost everything that happened against
Jews in this country

00:43:22.934 --> 00:43:24.955
happened against gays.

00:43:25.275 --> 00:43:26.435
And in the world, I might add.

00:43:27.907 --> 00:43:31.429
that you know it's all it's it's
it's history is written

00:43:31.469 --> 00:43:35.310
that that henry ford was anti-semitic
and tried to get rid of the jews

00:43:35.370 --> 00:43:39.472
it's not written that he hated gay people
and and and and did the same thing

00:43:39.832 --> 00:43:44.614
for them the things that i'm discovering
would would would literally blow your mind

00:43:45.774 --> 00:43:50.376
um there were concentration camps
in this country in this country

00:43:51.037 --> 00:43:57.059
in the wilderness for black people,
for lesbians, for gay people.

00:43:58.461 --> 00:44:01.926
I'm talking about
in the early part of the last century.

00:44:02.347 --> 00:44:03.469
Rich people who didn't like them.

00:44:05.131 --> 00:44:12.564
This country had mammoth, mammoth expanses
of land that nobody knew anything

00:44:12.604 --> 00:44:13.584
about or how to find it.

00:44:13.684 --> 00:44:19.486
And they would go and they'd put
100 black people in a camp that

00:44:19.507 --> 00:44:21.167
they couldn't get out of
and let them starve to death.

00:44:22.247 --> 00:44:22.988
Where was this?

00:44:23.608 --> 00:44:27.729
Texas, Arkansas, places like that.

00:44:27.849 --> 00:44:28.930
And who was organizing it?

00:44:29.810 --> 00:44:36.933
Well, a lot
of them were Mormons, people like that.

00:44:36.953 --> 00:44:38.253
And a lot of them were crazy people.

00:44:44.224 --> 00:44:46.866
the stuff that I'm finding out
and the stuff that people are sending to

00:44:46.906 --> 00:44:48.987
me now that they know I'm writing
this kind of a book

00:44:50.708 --> 00:44:52.469
how I found out the Abraham Lincoln stuff

00:44:57.393 --> 00:45:01.656
and it has the same

00:45:01.676 --> 00:45:13.380
and I guess this is what I got from
my mother it has the same um effect on me

00:45:13.980 --> 00:45:16.642
as how we were treated in the early days.

00:45:17.823 --> 00:45:18.623
It's wrong.

00:45:19.464 --> 00:45:20.625
It's just wrong.

00:45:22.205 --> 00:45:25.226
And that makes me very angry,
that I can't walk down

00:45:25.246 --> 00:45:27.588
the street holding David's hand is wrong.

00:45:28.468 --> 00:45:33.130
That we can't have those 1,000 benefits
that straight couples

00:45:33.150 --> 00:45:34.951
get when they're married is wrong.

00:45:35.711 --> 00:45:37.332
And that's what motivates me.

00:45:37.512 --> 00:45:46.316
And when that prick, I can't even remember
his name, Herb Rickman, who was

00:45:46.536 --> 00:45:51.645
Koch's gay assistant, his gay assistant,
hung up

00:45:51.745 --> 00:45:53.206
on me when I was trying to be nice.

00:45:53.226 --> 00:45:54.247
We don't want to know about this.

00:45:56.328 --> 00:46:00.830
A gay man working for a gay man,
telling a gay man

00:46:01.470 --> 00:46:05.172
that this city would not help us, wrong.

00:46:06.433 --> 00:46:07.714
You don't have to have political action.

00:46:08.154 --> 00:46:11.716
You don't have to think in the kind
of terms that you're, the dialectics that

00:46:11.736 --> 00:46:13.437
you're talking about
to know that it's wrong.

00:46:13.956 --> 00:46:16.897
You're talking about a certain kind
of tribal relationship

00:46:16.977 --> 00:46:20.398
that wherever gay people are,
wherever they're situated in

00:46:20.458 --> 00:46:23.158
any power struggle, they should have
an accountability to each other.

00:46:23.178 --> 00:46:24.098
Is that how you feel?

00:46:28.380 --> 00:46:30.380
Well, that's how it came to feel.

00:46:30.420 --> 00:46:36.402
In those days, I just thought,
you could die from this shit, too.

00:46:37.902 --> 00:46:38.242
Whatever.

00:46:41.704 --> 00:46:45.585
Yeah, I guess I got that
from being Jewish, tribal accountability.

00:46:45.605 --> 00:46:46.526
We all stuck together.

00:46:46.546 --> 00:46:50.606
I grew up in Washington,
not so many Jews there.

00:46:51.147 --> 00:46:54.787
I was the only Jewish kid in a number
of my schools, or one

00:46:54.807 --> 00:46:57.628
of a couple, until I got to high school.

00:47:05.183 --> 00:47:09.025
I knew that we were
safer than other people to be with.

00:47:10.346 --> 00:47:11.386
Yeah, good point.

00:47:12.307 --> 00:47:13.207
I remember that feeling.

00:47:13.588 --> 00:47:15.188
But do you operate that way yourself?

00:47:15.229 --> 00:47:18.310
Like if a gay person calls you
and asks for help, do you?

00:47:18.530 --> 00:47:19.131
Oh, God, yes.

00:47:19.431 --> 00:47:20.551
This is mission control here.

00:47:23.233 --> 00:47:27.135
And do you find that that ethic
has increased or diminished among

00:47:27.175 --> 00:47:30.797
gay people now in this era?

00:47:31.277 --> 00:47:45.396
I no longer talk about gay people
so much in terms of It ain't a community.

00:47:46.177 --> 00:47:46.597
No.

00:47:46.817 --> 00:47:47.498
Why do you say that?

00:47:50.780 --> 00:47:54.862
It's a population, and community is
too small

00:47:54.942 --> 00:47:56.883
and insular a word for what we are.

00:47:58.524 --> 00:48:02.346
And that began to become
increasingly evident and act up

00:48:02.466 --> 00:48:06.149
as we went along,
is that we couldn't support each other.

00:48:12.312 --> 00:48:13.973
I want to get back to the inside-outside.

00:48:14.013 --> 00:48:14.553
Who's that?

00:48:16.334 --> 00:48:19.095
Could you just go up?

00:48:20.055 --> 00:48:22.016
I just want to get back to,
you talked about one

00:48:22.036 --> 00:48:26.838
of the most important structural ideas
in ACT UP, which was

00:48:26.878 --> 00:48:28.839
the idea of an inside and an outside.

00:48:29.219 --> 00:48:32.240
That there would be people sitting in
meetings with pharmaceutical companies

00:48:32.300 --> 00:48:33.901
and government officials,
and then there'd be people

00:48:33.961 --> 00:48:34.621
out on the street.

00:48:35.515 --> 00:48:38.117
Well,
again, that was something that developed.

00:48:38.597 --> 00:48:41.999
I don't think anything
from day one was calculated.

00:48:42.239 --> 00:48:43.640
I've said it over and over again.

00:48:43.700 --> 00:48:45.321
We made it up as we went along.

00:48:45.921 --> 00:48:47.222
What were we being handed?

00:48:48.362 --> 00:48:48.802
Excuse me.

00:48:48.883 --> 00:48:49.303
Bless you.

00:48:49.743 --> 00:48:51.504
What were we being handed that day?

00:48:51.784 --> 00:48:53.105
What shit do we have to deal with?

00:48:54.617 --> 00:48:55.958
And it was all new to us.

00:48:55.998 --> 00:48:57.559
We didn't have experience in all of this.

00:48:57.819 --> 00:49:00.741
We didn't come from all
of these political backgrounds

00:49:01.501 --> 00:49:03.022
or service backgrounds or anything.

00:49:03.082 --> 00:49:09.013
We were mostly you know,
white and privileged.

00:49:11.094 --> 00:49:16.076
And there was a lot of flack against us
in the, quote, community because of that.

00:49:17.077 --> 00:49:18.318
Backed up, yeah, a lot.

00:49:18.418 --> 00:49:19.758
Same with GMHC.

00:49:19.778 --> 00:49:24.341
There was tons of flack against GMHC early
on that we were all white boys.

00:49:25.501 --> 00:49:30.544
Paul Popham and I used to go to
black churches and black organizations

00:49:30.584 --> 00:49:31.805
trying to get them involved.

00:49:32.705 --> 00:49:33.686
Didn't want to have anything
to do with it.

00:49:35.026 --> 00:49:36.487
So yes, we were all white.

00:49:40.015 --> 00:49:41.656
Anyway, we made it up as we went along.

00:49:42.456 --> 00:49:48.638
It was all pro, whatever the word is,
reactive to what the government was doing,

00:49:48.699 --> 00:49:55.281
or not doing, or doing in the city
and the state and the country.

00:49:55.321 --> 00:49:57.222
There was plenty to deal with,
and how are you going to deal with

00:49:57.262 --> 00:49:59.803
it with the limited resources we had?

00:49:59.883 --> 00:50:01.024
All we had were voices.

00:50:02.068 --> 00:50:04.270
All we had were a lot
of very creative people who could make

00:50:04.610 --> 00:50:09.015
beautiful posters
and had big healthy lungs.

00:50:09.095 --> 00:50:12.978
And because so many people
were sick, that gave us our spirit.

00:50:14.160 --> 00:50:14.640
What the fuck?

00:50:14.700 --> 00:50:16.161
They had nothing to do with it.

00:50:16.402 --> 00:50:19.845
As the strategy naturally evolved,
what do you think determined

00:50:19.905 --> 00:50:21.667
which group a person would end up in?

00:50:22.768 --> 00:50:23.028
Sorry?

00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:26.876
As this evolved,
what do you think would determine

00:50:27.137 --> 00:50:30.687
which element, the insider
or the outsider, a member

00:50:30.707 --> 00:50:32.272
of ACT UP would find themselves in?

00:50:33.260 --> 00:50:35.821
I think we were all both
at some point or another.

00:50:35.841 --> 00:50:37.242
I don't think it was black or white.

00:50:39.462 --> 00:50:44.004
Peter Staley, who was certainly
a good cop, that was a bad cop,

00:50:44.084 --> 00:50:45.565
locked himself inside Burroughs Welcome.

00:50:47.305 --> 00:50:48.706
I got arrested plenty of times, too.

00:50:49.026 --> 00:50:53.888
Do you think that all people
were involved in both aspects?

00:50:57.509 --> 00:50:59.010
Again, it wasn't so black and white.

00:51:02.423 --> 00:51:03.643
And it changed from day to day.

00:51:04.304 --> 00:51:07.624
And it was a long time
before the good cops

00:51:07.684 --> 00:51:08.905
could actually do anything.

00:51:10.225 --> 00:51:11.785
We were all bad cops in the beginning.

00:51:12.925 --> 00:51:19.087
And this is a bad terminology
because it connotes saintliness and devil.

00:51:19.767 --> 00:51:20.527
And it wasn't that.

00:51:20.907 --> 00:51:24.108
It was
just pressure versus honey or whatever.

00:51:25.268 --> 00:51:28.929
You said that ACT UP was criticized from
the community for being white.

00:51:29.602 --> 00:51:41.141
well, for being white, for being,
it became, as you well know, it became

00:51:41.181 --> 00:51:46.186
a place to go where all the hot guys were,
in their black jeans

00:51:46.307 --> 00:51:48.609
and their black boots.

00:51:49.270 --> 00:51:52.993
It became a look,
and those were unfortunately

00:51:53.033 --> 00:51:53.814
the people who were sick.

00:51:54.635 --> 00:51:58.079
But enough of them came,
so that gave us the image.

00:52:01.542 --> 00:52:05.647
There was a lot of criticism of that
by people who didn't look like that

00:52:05.708 --> 00:52:08.511
or didn't want to look like
that or were older.

00:52:08.871 --> 00:52:12.556
Older people felt unwelcome there
who probably wanted to do something.

00:52:12.576 --> 00:52:15.820
There weren't
that many older men there or women.

00:52:20.649 --> 00:52:24.051
And yes, there was criticism,
but it also gave us our image.

00:52:25.612 --> 00:52:30.316
I think the most successful demonstration
in terms of what it accomplished for

00:52:30.396 --> 00:52:31.917
us was the St. Patrick's Day thing.

00:52:32.857 --> 00:52:35.299
I remember going to the meeting after it.

00:52:35.339 --> 00:52:38.081
Everybody was terrified after it,
because it had been in the paper,

00:52:38.101 --> 00:52:41.763
and every editorial page
in town had dumped on

00:52:41.824 --> 00:52:43.345
us, and people were scared.

00:52:44.345 --> 00:52:46.227
And I remember saying, are you crazy?

00:52:46.928 --> 00:52:47.468
Are you crazy?

00:52:47.488 --> 00:52:48.289
They're afraid of us now.

00:52:48.909 --> 00:52:50.851
That's the best thing
that could ever have happened to us.

00:52:51.532 --> 00:52:52.252
And it was true.

00:52:53.093 --> 00:52:53.573
It was true.

00:52:54.875 --> 00:52:59.399
And... Do you think that being an ACT UP
gave you personally

00:52:59.539 --> 00:53:01.240
more access to the power system or less?

00:53:01.280 --> 00:53:01.941
Oh, God, yes.

00:53:02.421 --> 00:53:02.822
In what way?

00:53:02.842 --> 00:53:03.282
I remember...

00:53:04.103 --> 00:53:04.763
Oh, God, yes.

00:53:06.625 --> 00:53:06.845
I don't...

00:53:06.985 --> 00:53:08.487
I mean, it came slowly.

00:53:08.507 --> 00:53:10.989
I remember...

00:53:13.462 --> 00:53:18.484
When I was active in GMHC, I'd come home
and my answering machine would be

00:53:18.544 --> 00:53:21.685
all full, 30, 40 messages or whatever
they could hold in those days.

00:53:24.286 --> 00:53:26.847
The day I was thrown off the board

00:53:26.867 --> 00:53:31.349
and I came home, zero messages.

00:53:33.310 --> 00:53:34.050
Zero messages.

00:53:34.090 --> 00:53:35.711
And I said, ho, ho, ho.

00:53:36.951 --> 00:53:38.372
I had destroyed my soapbox.

00:53:40.332 --> 00:53:41.993
And I had to remake it somehow, slowly.

00:53:44.113 --> 00:53:45.354
Roger has a different view of all this.

00:53:45.374 --> 00:53:48.055
He thinks I subconsciously wanted
that to happen so I could write

00:53:48.075 --> 00:53:50.256
The Normal Heart, which I did.

00:53:51.777 --> 00:53:58.600
And I think that's giving
a little too much power

00:53:58.620 --> 00:54:00.001
to my creative unconscious.

00:54:00.041 --> 00:54:01.181
I don't think it was that powerful.

00:54:03.883 --> 00:54:04.503
What was your question?

00:54:05.383 --> 00:54:07.885
Did you get
more access to power as a result of that?

00:54:07.905 --> 00:54:12.627
Well, I come out
of the movie business, as I say.

00:54:13.368 --> 00:54:16.969
And I learned very early
on the power of publicity.

00:54:18.410 --> 00:54:23.592
And I always have felt
that the media was my friend.

00:54:24.432 --> 00:54:28.114
I used to say this to people,
make friends of the reporters.

00:54:29.214 --> 00:54:32.096
They may not be writing
what you want, but they can be useful.

00:54:32.756 --> 00:54:36.817
I cannot tell you to this day
how I can call so many people

00:54:36.877 --> 00:54:38.898
because we have become friends
over these years.

00:54:39.058 --> 00:54:40.099
I learned that in the movie business.

00:54:41.388 --> 00:54:47.231
I say to people who run not-for-profits,
why are you hiring communications people

00:54:47.371 --> 00:54:49.172
from other not-for-profits?

00:54:49.252 --> 00:54:51.253
Go to the movie business
and hire people from there.

00:54:51.293 --> 00:54:56.196
They know how to get stuff in the paper
that's got an attention-getting device.

00:54:59.098 --> 00:55:02.620
As I began to get more and more
of the publicity and had wrote more

00:55:02.640 --> 00:55:06.902
and more of my op-ed pages and wrote
The Normal Heart and wrote Reports

00:55:06.922 --> 00:55:11.265
on the Holocaust and all, then you become
sort of a, yes, people do call you

00:55:11.285 --> 00:55:13.647
and you do have that access
and it has been sustaining.

00:55:15.868 --> 00:55:19.910
Also a lot of everybody else is dead too,
so you get a lot of it by default.

00:55:20.311 --> 00:55:22.272
Do you think
that you personally wanted the attention?

00:55:28.866 --> 00:55:32.949
I don't look at it
or that or myself that way.

00:55:38.214 --> 00:55:41.456
The whole thing about ACT UP is results.

00:55:43.158 --> 00:55:44.379
That's all I was interested in.

00:55:44.399 --> 00:55:47.501
I was not
interested in airy-fairy theories.

00:55:48.923 --> 00:55:49.323
Results.

00:55:51.064 --> 00:55:52.305
How do we get these fucking drugs?

00:55:53.386 --> 00:55:54.427
That's what it was all about.

00:55:54.527 --> 00:55:55.148
How do we get them?

00:55:55.488 --> 00:55:57.850
And it was a slow process to getting them.

00:55:59.515 --> 00:56:03.698
And if I had to go out there and yell at
somebody, I made myself able to do that.

00:56:05.079 --> 00:56:08.201
Again, you may find it hard to believe,
but I am essentially a shy person.

00:56:10.782 --> 00:56:12.564
And it became like
a Jekyll and Hyde thing.

00:56:13.464 --> 00:56:19.128
And I took so much
of my energy from everyone else.

00:56:19.508 --> 00:56:20.529
That gave it to me.

00:56:21.650 --> 00:56:22.610
Could I do it by myself?

00:56:23.431 --> 00:56:25.292
I've learned how to,
but originally it was hard.

00:56:27.448 --> 00:56:32.613
Yes, it's given me—I can call people up
now, and yes, I can still get

00:56:32.653 --> 00:56:36.997
an op-ed piece in if I want,
but it isn't necessarily transferable.

00:56:38.198 --> 00:56:42.382
Case in point is
that I would like very much to be

00:56:42.422 --> 00:56:47.827
an activist for organ transplant,
because I've been so lucky there.

00:56:50.222 --> 00:56:51.903
Talk about
an issue nobody's interested in.

00:56:52.644 --> 00:56:55.566
It's just like AIDS in the early days.

00:56:56.167 --> 00:56:59.189
The numbers are nowhere comparable,
but the issue is just as bad.

00:57:04.941 --> 00:57:07.523
Now I know the head of the op-ed page.

00:57:09.344 --> 00:57:13.087
And I said, he actually called me,
would you like to write something?

00:57:13.127 --> 00:57:14.748
I said,
yes, what do you want to write about?

00:57:15.928 --> 00:57:17.229
I said, I would like to write
an organ transplant.

00:57:17.249 --> 00:57:18.410
He said, oh, do something on AIDS.

00:57:18.450 --> 00:57:19.811
I mean, that's what it comes down to.

00:57:21.492 --> 00:57:22.253
It's very hard.

00:57:24.594 --> 00:57:32.499
And again, the people who can be
your activists, by the time they know

00:57:32.539 --> 00:57:34.781
they need to be
an activist, it's too late.

00:57:35.822 --> 00:57:37.926
Okay,
let's just move on to some other things.

00:57:38.427 --> 00:57:41.493
I'm thinking a lot about the trajectory
of the development of drugs, and I know

00:57:41.513 --> 00:57:42.835
that you were very involved in that.

00:57:44.505 --> 00:57:47.908
I remember, you know, there was a time
at the beginning when people put faith

00:57:47.948 --> 00:57:49.489
in a lot of drugs that didn't pan out.

00:57:50.750 --> 00:57:55.054
You know, dextran sulfate,
AL-721, all of that stuff.

00:57:55.714 --> 00:57:58.416
And I even remember when you came in
and said, they're dancing in the streets

00:57:58.476 --> 00:58:04.261
in San Francisco, compound Q.
When the protease inhibitors started to,

00:58:04.761 --> 00:58:08.784
when the vision of protease inhibitors
came to be, did you know instinctively

00:58:08.824 --> 00:58:10.786
that that was going to be successful
or were you concerned

00:58:10.806 --> 00:58:13.688
that it was going to end up
like the previous wishes?

00:58:19.384 --> 00:58:24.789
Oh, by the time the proteases come,
now you're talking about the late 90s.

00:58:26.270 --> 00:58:28.292
Act up is all intents and purposes.

00:58:28.312 --> 00:58:28.972
There's no more.

00:58:30.333 --> 00:58:41.725
And I think, and to this day still think,
is that you... you hope for

00:58:41.745 --> 00:58:43.107
the best and expect the worst.

00:58:43.467 --> 00:58:46.851
That was something
that's always been my philosophy.

00:58:47.711 --> 00:58:51.555
The days of AL-721 and all that,
I didn't think it was

00:58:52.757 --> 00:58:55.840
so wrong to explore all these things.

00:58:56.300 --> 00:59:04.412
And I don't want to say false hope is
better than no hope, but We didn't know

00:59:04.432 --> 00:59:08.320
it was false hope, you know,
if AL-721 came out of

00:59:08.540 --> 00:59:10.324
the Weizmann Institute,
for crying out loud.

00:59:13.366 --> 00:59:13.986
you go with it.

00:59:14.346 --> 00:59:19.450
Compound Q came out of San Francisco
and Marty Delaney, whom I'm still

00:59:19.490 --> 00:59:22.532
very close to, and they believed in it.

00:59:23.712 --> 00:59:24.333
And it did help.

00:59:25.353 --> 00:59:26.794
Would you believe
there's still people on it?

00:59:27.875 --> 00:59:28.936
And it did help some people.

00:59:30.617 --> 00:59:32.998
I knew instinctively
from the beginning AZT was no good.

00:59:33.959 --> 00:59:34.719
I did say that.

00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:38.402
Was there a fight inside ACT UP about AZT?

00:59:40.934 --> 00:59:46.417
No, what there was was about the dosing.

00:59:48.118 --> 00:59:51.740
We were right, TND was right,
that they were giving too much.

00:59:53.521 --> 00:59:58.804
I forgot they were giving, I don't know,
a thousand milligrams a day and really all

00:59:58.824 --> 01:00:02.225
you needed was two
or three hundred, four at the most.

01:00:03.646 --> 01:00:07.608
That was
because of ACT UP that that happened.

01:00:09.389 --> 01:00:10.450
So I don't know, fights.

01:00:11.831 --> 01:00:13.012
That wasn't within ACT UP.

01:00:13.152 --> 01:00:17.114
It was
ACT UP versus probably by then the NIH.

01:00:18.355 --> 01:00:20.557
I just want to ask you a little bit
about yourself as a person

01:00:20.577 --> 01:00:22.478
with AIDS and the ACT UP context.

01:00:23.098 --> 01:00:25.640
When did you begin to think
that you were positive?

01:00:25.720 --> 01:00:26.961
At what point in all of this?

01:00:27.061 --> 01:00:28.882
I theoretically still don't have AIDS.

01:00:30.303 --> 01:00:38.208
I've never had a defining illness,
and I've never had low enough markers.

01:00:39.789 --> 01:00:41.030
I am the luckiest man alive.

01:00:41.050 --> 01:00:47.171
I never had to take
any HIV drugs until I got my liver.

01:00:51.392 --> 01:00:55.714
And the only reason I had to take them was
because the transplant people

01:00:55.774 --> 01:00:57.094
insisted to protect the liver.

01:00:57.134 --> 01:01:00.355
They wanted to keep HIV in check
whether it was out of check or not.

01:01:02.333 --> 01:01:07.177
So you've been HIV positive, asymptomatic?

01:01:07.197 --> 01:01:09.719
Since 80, I forgot
when I was tested already, 85, 86.

01:01:12.221 --> 01:01:14.022
And why do you think
you were asymptomatic?

01:01:17.025 --> 01:01:17.565
I am lucky.

01:01:17.585 --> 01:01:18.406
I have no idea.

01:01:19.066 --> 01:01:19.647
I have no idea.

01:01:19.687 --> 01:01:23.009
Everybody, not everybody,
but almost everybody

01:01:23.049 --> 01:01:24.971
I knew is dead from those years.

01:01:27.912 --> 01:01:30.175
Do you think it's a strain issue
or do you think

01:01:30.215 --> 01:01:31.977
it's a genetic predisposition?

01:01:31.997 --> 01:01:32.517
I don't know.

01:01:35.961 --> 01:01:36.382
I don't know.

01:01:46.969 --> 01:01:49.690
You don't know how close I came to dying
a couple years ago

01:01:49.930 --> 01:01:52.572
because of the hepatitis B in my liver.

01:01:53.552 --> 01:01:54.853
I was given six months to live.

01:01:54.873 --> 01:01:57.414
If you remember, I looked like this.

01:01:57.434 --> 01:02:01.036
I had no energy.

01:02:01.856 --> 01:02:05.458
They told me that was the end
because livers were not available.

01:02:11.994 --> 01:02:15.158
And the days were ticking away.

01:02:16.679 --> 01:02:23.006
Just prior to that, Dr. Fauci,
the man I had called a murderer

01:02:24.408 --> 01:02:28.452
many years before,
has become one of my closest friends.

01:02:28.753 --> 01:02:30.935
Talk about a moving story of irony.

01:02:33.752 --> 01:02:35.694
He saw me somewhere
and he said, you look terrible.

01:02:35.754 --> 01:02:39.077
And he put me in the NIH hospital
and they discovered a lot of this shit

01:02:39.217 --> 01:02:41.019
that had not been discovered in me before.

01:02:42.280 --> 01:02:44.882
And there was
a hepatitis B experimental drug

01:02:44.962 --> 01:02:45.763
in trial there.

01:02:47.224 --> 01:02:53.770
And so I got the, it was called a Defavir
and that calmed down my liver for a while.

01:02:55.252 --> 01:02:57.774
But then it stopped calming down the liver
and that's

01:02:57.814 --> 01:02:59.936
when I only had six months to live.

01:03:01.696 --> 01:03:04.877
And I was down there one day
to pick up the medicine.

01:03:05.157 --> 01:03:06.858
You had to go there once a month
to get the medicine.

01:03:08.919 --> 01:03:13.901
And my doctor down there, a woman called
Judy Falloon, said, I think you may be

01:03:13.961 --> 01:03:15.501
eligible for a liver transplant.

01:03:18.002 --> 01:03:21.043
The minute she said that, I knew
I was going to get that fucking liver.

01:03:21.063 --> 01:03:21.883
I just knew it.

01:03:22.404 --> 01:03:23.104
I just knew it.

01:03:25.322 --> 01:03:26.743
She didn't say how I could get it.

01:03:26.883 --> 01:03:27.884
She said I had to apply.

01:03:27.964 --> 01:03:32.929
They were just beginning to transplant
people with HIV

01:03:33.010 --> 01:03:35.272
and hep B co-infectants, we recall.

01:03:36.513 --> 01:03:41.118
There was, in fact, an NIH trial out of
San Francisco with Michelle Rowland,

01:03:41.218 --> 01:03:44.441
our old active lady
out there, putting it together.

01:03:48.146 --> 01:03:49.168
wanted people like me.

01:03:49.829 --> 01:03:51.110
So that's how I got the liver.

01:03:51.531 --> 01:03:52.833
I didn't get it
because I was Larry Kramer.

01:03:52.873 --> 01:03:54.796
I did it because they had this trial
that was just starting

01:03:54.836 --> 01:03:56.118
and nobody wanted to go into it.

01:03:57.039 --> 01:03:58.842
But now wait,
what year did you test positive?

01:03:59.938 --> 01:04:01.138
Oh, that's a long time before.

01:04:01.439 --> 01:04:06.420
I tested positive for HIV in,
I can't remember, 85,

01:04:06.120 --> 01:04:09.321
86, 87, somewhere in there.

01:04:10.361 --> 01:04:10.762
Before.

01:04:11.062 --> 01:04:13.803
But I knew
I'd had hep B from the late 70s.

01:04:13.883 --> 01:04:14.383
Then I knew.

01:04:15.803 --> 01:04:20.925
At that era, a lot
of people were advocating for

01:04:21.025 --> 01:04:23.166
early medication before symptom.

01:04:23.206 --> 01:04:24.206
How did you resist that?

01:04:25.486 --> 01:04:27.647
Because my doctor, Jeff Green,
said, I don't think you need it.

01:04:28.947 --> 01:04:31.268
We had ordered Crixivan,
which was the first one out.

01:04:32.068 --> 01:04:34.629
And I had the bottle in my hand,
and we were going to start.

01:04:35.550 --> 01:04:37.831
And he called me up
and he said, let's wait a while.

01:04:38.311 --> 01:04:39.171
I said, fine with me.

01:04:41.092 --> 01:04:42.092
So what meds are you taking?

01:04:42.112 --> 01:04:43.673
Which HIV meds are you taking?

01:04:43.693 --> 01:04:45.714
I took...

01:04:48.894 --> 01:04:54.218
I took AZT when my liver started going bad
and my platelets go down.

01:04:54.298 --> 01:04:58.480
AZT, unknown to a lot of people,
also raises your platelets.

01:04:58.761 --> 01:04:59.621
So I took it for that.

01:05:01.142 --> 01:05:05.785
And then I took Epivir for 3TC,
whatever it's called, for the hep A.

01:05:06.085 --> 01:05:08.047
And that's all
I took for a number of years.

01:05:09.307 --> 01:05:14.731
And then when the transplant
started, I've taken a bunch of them.

01:05:14.771 --> 01:05:18.730
I took... virus
up until I became resistant to it.

01:05:18.770 --> 01:05:26.036
I took Sustiva, which I loathed,
until fortunately I became

01:05:26.096 --> 01:05:28.297
resistant to it because it drove me nuts.

01:05:29.218 --> 01:05:35.222
And now I'm on something called,
which is an amazing drug because it has

01:05:35.282 --> 01:05:36.303
absolutely no side effects.

01:05:36.423 --> 01:05:37.304
It's like taking an aspirin.

01:05:37.324 --> 01:05:39.085
It's called Rayataz.

01:05:40.646 --> 01:05:47.126
And only now they've just discovered
Steve Miles, the UCLA AIDS man,

01:05:47.166 --> 01:05:52.693
has just discovered that Rayataz
interacting with the other drugs

01:05:52.753 --> 01:05:54.856
I take is bad for hepatitis B.

01:05:56.138 --> 01:06:02.265
So we know so much now, and I have
so many doctors that I I mean,

01:06:03.005 --> 01:06:04.447
talk about patient empowerment.

01:06:05.207 --> 01:06:06.349
I brought it to a new art.

01:06:07.029 --> 01:06:12.294
I have six doctors
who get—I email everything about me,

01:06:12.474 --> 01:06:13.876
and I pester them all to death.

01:06:15.097 --> 01:06:20.682
And I take advantage of everything
I possibly can to get the information

01:06:20.742 --> 01:06:22.844
I need,
just what we advocated everybody to do.

01:06:23.965 --> 01:06:29.310
They don't like that I do
this group email, and not one

01:06:29.330 --> 01:06:33.414
of them will, when they answer me,
will copy all the others out of courtesy.

01:06:33.434 --> 01:06:35.296
They only send it to me,
and I've got to send it around.

01:06:36.377 --> 01:06:39.680
Too many cooks, Larry, too many cooks.

01:06:40.200 --> 01:06:44.044
Which of these drugs do you feel
exists as a consequence of ACT UP?

01:06:44.344 --> 01:06:44.764
All of them.

01:06:46.106 --> 01:06:48.688
I have no doubt in my mind
those fucking drugs

01:06:48.708 --> 01:06:49.769
are out there because of ACT UP.

01:06:51.792 --> 01:06:53.817
And that's
our greatest, greatest achievement.

01:06:55.003 --> 01:06:55.463
Totally.

01:06:56.443 --> 01:07:01.884
From that day, Iris Long came in
and started teaching the pharmacology

01:07:01.904 --> 01:07:06.325
of everything, whatever was known,
or what little was known about the virus.

01:07:07.925 --> 01:07:09.986
Through all of that.

01:07:11.026 --> 01:07:12.846
We had guys
teaching us civil disobedience.

01:07:12.866 --> 01:07:13.846
I don't know if you remember that.

01:07:13.906 --> 01:07:18.087
We had some guy, some big black guy
who had done something

01:07:18.107 --> 01:07:21.888
for Martin Luther King or something, came
and gave us civil disobedience lessons

01:07:21.928 --> 01:07:27.634
about how to lie down in the street
and let your body go limp and all that.

01:07:28.335 --> 01:07:28.896
All of those things.

01:07:28.916 --> 01:07:30.898
We taught ourselves everything,
everything.

01:07:30.918 --> 01:07:34.123
I didn't go to a meeting,
I didn't know what the NIH was until,

01:07:34.503 --> 01:07:43.635
I don't know when it was,
After the Normal Heart, I think.

01:07:43.355 --> 01:07:49.038
86, I don't know, a guy called
Tim Westmoreland, who was a wonderful man

01:07:49.118 --> 01:07:54.902
who we owe a lot to, a gay man
who worked at the time for Senator Waxman,

01:07:55.182 --> 01:07:58.044
who was the only guy
in government who was our friend.

01:07:59.085 --> 01:08:02.747
And he said,
you really ought to go to visit.

01:08:03.367 --> 01:08:04.468
Yeah, it was before the Normal Heart.

01:08:08.484 --> 01:08:11.765
go and visit the NIH,
" and he set up this meeting for me there.

01:08:12.505 --> 01:08:17.226
This was before Fauci was head of NIAID.

01:08:18.506 --> 01:08:19.487
Oh, this famous story.

01:08:20.807 --> 01:08:26.008
All the heads of the various institutes
can live in their own houses.

01:08:26.068 --> 01:08:27.989
It looks like a college campus.

01:08:28.049 --> 01:08:28.629
It's very pretty.

01:08:30.069 --> 01:08:33.270
The head of NIAID was
a guy called Crousey, Richard Crousey.

01:08:34.810 --> 01:08:37.091
And he invited us, invited me,
I was there by myself,

01:08:37.851 --> 01:08:40.132
and my dog Molly, to dinner, to lunch.

01:08:42.172 --> 01:08:45.794
And his assistant
was a guy called Jack Whitescarver.

01:08:47.854 --> 01:08:54.877
And they gave me all this bullshit about,
you know, there was money for this

01:08:54.897 --> 01:08:57.337
and money for that, and this is happening,
and blah, blah, blah, column A

01:08:57.377 --> 01:08:59.218
and column B,
and I called them on everything.

01:09:00.891 --> 01:09:03.815
And he had to leave,
and the dishes were all there.

01:09:03.895 --> 01:09:08.782
So Jack White's carver
and I washed the dishes in this house.

01:09:09.283 --> 01:09:10.244
And I had to go to the john.

01:09:11.326 --> 01:09:12.107
And I went upstairs.

01:09:12.127 --> 01:09:13.549
There was
only one john on the second floor.

01:09:15.814 --> 01:09:21.397
And coming out of the john, I look into
this bedroom, and there are bookcases

01:09:21.457 --> 01:09:24.900
and things and photos all around,
so what writer isn't nosy?

01:09:24.940 --> 01:09:25.800
This one certainly is.

01:09:25.820 --> 01:09:31.984
So I go in the bedroom,
and on Krause's bureau are pictures of him

01:09:32.964 --> 01:09:34.806
with all these guys in bathing suits.

01:09:35.406 --> 01:09:39.088
And I say,
holy fucking shit, this guy is gay.

01:09:41.670 --> 01:09:49.554
And I go downstairs, and I say,
White's carer whispers to me, no one there

01:09:49.574 --> 01:09:53.097
but him and me, he says, I want
you to know that my friend

01:09:53.137 --> 01:09:54.838
and I just loved faggots.

01:09:56.740 --> 01:10:00.482
So I looked him in the eye
and I said, Jack, is Krause gay?

01:10:04.966 --> 01:10:05.406
No sound.

01:10:07.392 --> 01:10:10.233
So once again,
we're crucified by our very own.

01:10:10.914 --> 01:10:14.695
You've got Koch in New York City.

01:10:15.096 --> 01:10:17.837
You've got Krause at the head
of the NIAID, which is

01:10:17.857 --> 01:10:22.699
the most important institute at the NIH
for looking after infectious diseases.

01:10:23.079 --> 01:10:24.660
And we've got this prick in
the White House who's got

01:10:24.700 --> 01:10:29.982
a supposedly gay son
who— Do you still think he's gay?

01:10:30.022 --> 01:10:30.723
Oh, I know he's gay.

01:10:33.046 --> 01:10:34.827
All right, well then let's take
that paradigm

01:10:34.847 --> 01:10:37.128
and let's move it to show business, okay?

01:10:37.148 --> 01:10:37.448
Yes, okay.

01:10:38.048 --> 01:10:41.849
So in terms of science and politics,
we have gay people who are telling

01:10:41.869 --> 01:10:45.291
the truth about what gay people's lives
are like and who are uniting together

01:10:45.311 --> 01:10:48.072
and who are changing the world
and saving their own lives.

01:10:48.532 --> 01:10:51.133
And then you have gay people in positions
of enormous power

01:10:51.653 --> 01:10:54.094
who are not at all dealing with
the reality

01:10:54.114 --> 01:10:55.095
of what's happening with gay people.

01:10:57.392 --> 01:11:01.614
Now let's put that
into the Hollywood arts representation

01:11:01.654 --> 01:11:02.254
category.

01:11:02.274 --> 01:11:07.956
Okay, your play The Normal Heart
is probably, and I know,

01:11:07.976 --> 01:11:13.779
I'm just guessing this, but I think it is
the only work of art that actually shows

01:11:13.899 --> 01:11:18.361
homophobic cruelty against gay people
that has been acclaimed

01:11:18.441 --> 01:11:19.681
and made it into the mainstream.

01:11:20.762 --> 01:11:24.223
Any other time gay people make that kind
of work, it remains underground.

01:11:25.845 --> 01:11:28.047
And most of the representations
of gay life

01:11:28.467 --> 01:11:32.350
and of AIDS really are predicated on
showing a certain kind

01:11:32.450 --> 01:11:37.834
of heroic heterosexual who overcomes
their prejudices to benevolently help

01:11:37.894 --> 01:11:39.916
the poor, alone, isolated gay people.

01:11:40.736 --> 01:11:42.578
And if you look at most work
about gay people

01:11:42.638 --> 01:11:44.139
and AIDS, there is no movement.

01:11:44.999 --> 01:11:49.062
The story that you just told about ACT UP
creating the drugs that

01:11:49.363 --> 01:11:55.646
you are taking today, that story is not
present in commercial representations

01:11:55.766 --> 01:11:58.827
of AIDS and homosexuality, even
though many of those stories

01:11:58.907 --> 01:12:00.427
are made by gay people themselves.

01:12:02.248 --> 01:12:03.548
So how do you account for that?

01:12:10.089 --> 01:12:11.609
How many millions of us are there?

01:12:13.130 --> 01:12:15.170
Tell me, I don't know, 10, 20?

01:12:18.751 --> 01:12:20.611
I believe
Jews walked into the gas chambers.

01:12:22.142 --> 01:12:23.003
That's what I believe.

01:12:23.303 --> 01:12:26.845
I believe they walked willingly
into their own destructions.

01:12:28.065 --> 01:12:29.586
How did six million people do that?

01:12:31.647 --> 01:12:36.069
I'm reading now so widely
into the literature of those times,

01:12:36.710 --> 01:12:39.231
but they did, over
and over and over again.

01:12:39.871 --> 01:12:40.772
Who walked them in?

01:12:41.272 --> 01:12:41.813
Other Jews.

01:12:43.633 --> 01:12:48.116
Mengele's chief right-hand surgeon
at Auschwitz was a Jew.

01:12:49.324 --> 01:12:54.346
So why, so gay people
who misrepresent the reality of gay life.

01:12:54.366 --> 01:12:55.887
Nobody took anybody with them.

01:12:56.527 --> 01:13:01.849
Nobody, nobody, nobody to kill one person
was willing to kill or lose his own life.

01:13:02.789 --> 01:13:06.871
So, but today we see so many gay people
involved in false representations

01:13:06.911 --> 01:13:08.352
of homosexuality and AIDS.

01:13:08.412 --> 01:13:09.172
Why are they doing it?

01:13:10.134 --> 01:13:11.075
Do they know they're doing it?

01:13:11.135 --> 01:13:11.595
I don't know.

01:13:13.116 --> 01:13:17.139
You're cursed with
too perceptive a critical mind

01:13:17.179 --> 01:13:17.699
in all of this.

01:13:18.219 --> 01:13:20.601
People don't want to make waves.

01:13:21.222 --> 01:13:22.743
I don't know.

01:13:22.783 --> 01:13:25.444
Reports on the Holocaust is filled with
one essay after another

01:13:25.484 --> 01:13:29.287
trying to understand why all of this goes
on, and in the end you have to throw up

01:13:29.327 --> 01:13:30.428
your hands and say, I don't know.

01:13:30.988 --> 01:13:34.854
I don't know why 10 million men
or 20 million men at the height

01:13:34.874 --> 01:13:39.140
of the plague didn't fight to save
their own lives to this day.

01:13:39.360 --> 01:13:45.830
And believe me, I told every one
of them, I was barred from Fire Island.

01:13:47.165 --> 01:13:49.447
People stopped inviting me to parties.

01:13:49.487 --> 01:13:54.410
To this day, I'm not on many social lists
in the gay world in New York.

01:13:54.751 --> 01:13:57.873
Well, do you think that there's, okay,
but do you think that right now we're not

01:13:57.913 --> 01:14:00.535
at the height of the AIDS crisis,
so the life and death issue

01:14:00.615 --> 01:14:01.936
is constructed a bit differently.

01:14:02.496 --> 01:14:04.197
So there's got to be something else
at stake.

01:14:04.578 --> 01:14:07.440
Do you think that gay men just want
straight men's approval and they're

01:14:07.460 --> 01:14:09.481
willing to tell
a story that isn't true to get it?

01:14:09.721 --> 01:14:10.602
Or is it about money?

01:14:10.802 --> 01:14:11.543
What is the motive?

01:14:12.771 --> 01:14:13.352
I don't know.

01:14:14.313 --> 01:14:17.496
I don't think they want
straight men's approval.

01:14:18.998 --> 01:14:22.061
I think
people just don't want to make waves.

01:14:23.723 --> 01:14:24.403
Leave me alone.

01:14:25.525 --> 01:14:26.386
Life is hard enough.

01:14:28.658 --> 01:14:29.338
I want to go home.

01:14:30.659 --> 01:14:33.200
You know, straight people
don't want to do it either.

01:14:33.940 --> 01:14:36.021
You show me
a movement that's any different.

01:14:36.882 --> 01:14:38.643
You show me
a population that's any different.

01:14:39.843 --> 01:14:44.766
Talk about some, I was, where was I,
at Harvard at the Kennedy School lecturing

01:14:44.786 --> 01:14:48.347
a couple weeks ago,
and this beautiful reporter

01:14:48.367 --> 01:14:54.291
from Kenya woman asked me, What message
did I have for her people

01:14:54.311 --> 01:14:57.012
who were in such terrible straits?

01:14:57.053 --> 01:15:00.355
It wasn't Kenya, it was somewhere else,
one of those countries.

01:15:02.096 --> 01:15:03.637
I said, you're not going to like what
I have to say.

01:15:07.860 --> 01:15:08.900
Get out there and do something.

01:15:09.361 --> 01:15:10.521
Don't take it lying down.

01:15:12.042 --> 01:15:15.970
Throw blood at somebody sit in
on somebody, close down

01:15:15.990 --> 01:15:18.511
a hospital, throw, you know, whatever.

01:15:19.212 --> 01:15:21.273
You have only yourself to fight for.

01:15:21.393 --> 01:15:23.154
Why is everybody
so passive in all of this?

01:15:24.494 --> 01:15:26.255
And I say it to gay people to this day.

01:15:26.275 --> 01:15:28.036
I mean,
we still have this gay marriage thing.

01:15:28.056 --> 01:15:32.358
I'm so sick of all of these people
who say, oh, I don't want to get married.

01:15:32.418 --> 01:15:36.080
I don't believe in gay marriage
because I don't want to perpetuate

01:15:36.120 --> 01:15:37.621
a straight blah, blah.

01:15:39.901 --> 01:15:41.222
It's more pragmatic than that.

01:15:41.262 --> 01:15:46.926
There are 1,003, or whatever the number is
that NGLTF or whatever it was then came up

01:15:46.986 --> 01:15:49.708
with, benefits,
tangible financial benefits that

01:15:49.749 --> 01:15:52.991
you get from the government if you are
married that straight people get.

01:15:54.332 --> 01:15:55.533
And I now face all of that.

01:15:55.613 --> 01:15:58.735
I have a lover I want to leave in
my estate, or he wants to leave me

01:15:58.775 --> 01:16:02.498
his estate without whatever
the excruciating taxes are

01:16:03.199 --> 01:16:05.280
that a straight couple
doesn't have to pay.

01:16:06.501 --> 01:16:07.462
That's worth fighting for.

01:16:09.125 --> 01:16:14.306
All these people who are adopting babies,
there are a lot of rights there

01:16:14.346 --> 01:16:15.286
involved that we don't have.

01:16:16.407 --> 01:16:18.287
So don't give me
that you don't want to perpetuate a

01:16:18.327 --> 01:16:23.308
terrible heterosexual, whatever
it's called, ceremony or something.

01:16:24.029 --> 01:16:27.969
You don't want to call it a wedding,
call it a, whatever,

01:16:28.009 --> 01:16:28.870
just give me those rights.

01:16:30.450 --> 01:16:31.390
Larry, I want to ask you.

01:16:31.770 --> 01:16:32.891
Maybe we should change tapes.

01:16:32.951 --> 01:16:33.891
Oh, you need to change tapes?

01:16:34.051 --> 01:16:34.351
Okay.

01:16:37.185 --> 01:16:38.346
Doesn't everybody talk like this?

01:16:38.786 --> 01:16:38.986
No?

01:16:42.708 --> 01:16:46.471
You know, as long as I've known you,
I've always responded the same way.

01:16:46.531 --> 01:16:49.292
I agree with half of what you say,
sometimes within the sentence.

01:16:50.973 --> 01:16:51.874
What don't you agree with?

01:16:54.475 --> 01:16:55.396
That's not helpful.

01:16:57.797 --> 01:16:59.698
I think that they're telling
straight people what straight people

01:16:59.738 --> 01:17:03.721
want to hear about AIDS and homosexuality,
and I think they get rewarded for it.

01:17:04.810 --> 01:17:08.534
People do, why are you oppositional
and other people go along with it?

01:17:08.554 --> 01:17:10.876
It has something to do with
your psychology and development.

01:17:11.156 --> 01:17:11.416
Okay.

01:17:11.436 --> 01:17:13.238
All right, let's start with this.

01:17:13.258 --> 01:17:13.678
Go Larry.

01:17:13.698 --> 01:17:14.019
Okay.

01:17:31.741 --> 01:17:33.521
Those reasons
are as good as any, I don't know.

01:17:35.802 --> 01:17:37.562
I don't give
a shit what straight people think.

01:17:38.322 --> 01:17:41.103
And I don't give a shit what
other gay people think for the most part.

01:17:42.643 --> 01:17:47.284
And it's a lesson that I've, it's been
a hard lesson to learn not

01:17:47.304 --> 01:17:48.365
to care what other people think.

01:17:48.385 --> 01:17:50.825
It was probably one of the hardest lessons
I ever had to learn.

01:17:51.525 --> 01:17:57.327
And I learned it long
before all of this started to happen.

01:17:57.507 --> 01:17:58.967
But it's the best thing I ever learned.

01:18:02.287 --> 01:18:11.654
I think that it is so hard to get anything
that you have created out there.

01:18:12.995 --> 01:18:18.019
It is so hard to get a job, to get
a screenwriting job, to get anything

01:18:18.059 --> 01:18:19.280
made, to get a book published.

01:18:23.203 --> 01:18:29.468
And some of it has nothing to do with
the talent or the worth of the project.

01:18:31.900 --> 01:18:37.101
It's just the nature of
how we've been condemned as a people.

01:18:38.742 --> 01:18:45.323
And most people don't have the skills that
I have, that I've learned,

01:18:45.983 --> 01:18:50.484
that I've taught, or that I've forged in
myself through all of this, to scream

01:18:50.545 --> 01:18:54.045
and yell and kick and get attention
and call you names and whatever.

01:18:55.526 --> 01:18:56.486
And they don't know how to do it.

01:18:58.314 --> 01:19:04.880
And you have to do it from a position
of having nothing to lose.

01:19:07.455 --> 01:19:09.296
which fortunately I'm in.

01:19:10.156 --> 01:19:11.597
I don't have to worry about
making a living.

01:19:12.598 --> 01:19:15.059
Very valuable gift
my brother gave me, exceedingly.

01:19:16.300 --> 01:19:21.722
And I know that I've been lucky,
so I feel that's part of my responsibility

01:19:21.742 --> 01:19:25.184
to honor all of that by doing all of this.

01:19:25.224 --> 01:19:27.465
But people don't have that.

01:19:27.485 --> 01:19:29.527
I hated Philadelphia.

01:19:29.587 --> 01:19:30.107
I hated it.

01:19:30.907 --> 01:19:35.711
And I said so in print, in an article
that was reprinted all over the country.

01:19:36.651 --> 01:19:40.934
And I hated Ron Niswander
or whatever the guy's name who wrote it.

01:19:42.095 --> 01:19:43.997
And I hated them all for making it.

01:19:44.457 --> 01:19:46.438
And I loathed the movie.

01:19:49.280 --> 01:19:50.741
But I come out of...

01:19:51.542 --> 01:19:52.643
It'll just...

01:19:56.305 --> 01:19:57.346
Are we going to hear the message?

01:19:57.366 --> 01:19:57.466
Yes.

01:19:58.829 --> 01:19:59.269
I don't know.

01:19:59.810 --> 01:20:02.952
Why don't you just pick it up
and just say— You want to just pick it up?

01:20:03.232 --> 01:20:04.432
Who is it, and can I call you back?

01:20:05.193 --> 01:20:05.493
Hello?

01:20:07.354 --> 01:20:08.735
No, can I take a message?

01:20:11.857 --> 01:20:12.337
Who's calling?

01:20:12.898 --> 01:20:13.778
I'm being interviewed.

01:20:14.058 --> 01:20:14.699
It's David Webster.

01:20:15.019 --> 01:20:16.180
Yeah, tell him I'm being interviewed.

01:20:16.220 --> 01:20:16.560
What's up?

01:20:16.580 --> 01:20:17.760
We're in the middle of an interview.

01:20:17.781 --> 01:20:18.581
He wants to know what's up.

01:20:19.061 --> 01:20:19.401
Being filmed.

01:20:19.562 --> 01:20:20.442
Relating this to Larry.

01:20:21.243 --> 01:20:21.483
Yes.

01:20:26.670 --> 01:20:29.833
Did you get something for the people
that you were going to dinner with?

01:20:29.853 --> 01:20:30.033
No.

01:20:30.814 --> 01:20:31.014
No.

01:20:35.078 --> 01:20:37.060
Okay, we'll see you later.

01:20:37.180 --> 01:20:38.721
Okay.

01:20:38.741 --> 01:20:39.222
There you go.

01:20:39.778 --> 01:20:41.661
What do you think would have to change for
the-

01:20:41.681 --> 01:20:43.924
I just know how hard it is for them.

01:20:44.024 --> 01:20:47.248
I, like all the rest of us, went after
Barry Diller and David Geffen

01:20:47.268 --> 01:20:49.931
and all these people
in the movie business forever.

01:20:50.292 --> 01:20:52.234
And did they change?

01:20:54.057 --> 01:20:55.519
David Geffen did, Barry Diller didn't.

01:20:59.158 --> 01:21:03.061
Well, if the true story of AIDS is
that gay people were despised

01:21:03.101 --> 01:21:07.644
and abandoned, and people with AIDS
and gay people and some straight women

01:21:07.664 --> 01:21:12.427
like Iris Long united together
and built a movement and were brilliant

01:21:12.627 --> 01:21:16.350
and changed the world and saved
their own lives, if that is the story,

01:21:16.730 --> 01:21:19.913
how long will it take before
that story gets told in a mainstream way?

01:21:19.933 --> 01:21:21.414
Somebody's got to write it.

01:21:23.395 --> 01:21:25.737
Do you think that the... Go ahead.

01:21:26.017 --> 01:21:26.037
I...

01:21:26.744 --> 01:21:28.985
I'm writing a certain amount
of it, but not that specific.

01:21:29.185 --> 01:21:30.186
Nobody's written about it.

01:21:31.546 --> 01:21:37.649
I got Mark Harrington a book deal
after the fast track thing was fashioned

01:21:37.689 --> 01:21:42.352
because of us, when we were still talking.

01:21:43.272 --> 01:21:43.892
He wrote the book.

01:21:47.621 --> 01:21:49.502
And it was never published.

01:21:49.542 --> 01:21:51.183
I was never given the opportunity
to read it

01:21:51.223 --> 01:21:52.564
because by then we'd stopped talking.

01:21:53.324 --> 01:21:56.526
So I don't know if it was any good,
but it was indeed about the mechanism

01:21:56.566 --> 01:21:58.627
of all of this
and how we had fought through all of this.

01:21:58.867 --> 01:22:02.129
Do you think that the culture
is ready to face the truth of that story?

01:22:03.693 --> 01:22:05.814
I don't think people give a shit about us.

01:22:05.874 --> 01:22:08.134
You talk about
it as if they're doing it intentionally.

01:22:08.995 --> 01:22:09.735
Some of them are.

01:22:09.775 --> 01:22:12.736
You know, the Jesse Helmses
and the right-wing blah-blahs are.

01:22:13.396 --> 01:22:15.577
But most people really don't care.

01:22:15.637 --> 01:22:17.497
It's
like I don't give a shit about football.

01:22:20.138 --> 01:22:21.538
And I'm sure a lot of people,
I have

01:22:21.558 --> 01:22:22.779
a lot of gay friends who love football.

01:22:22.819 --> 01:22:25.540
But I mean, talk about
something that puts me to sleep.

01:22:27.160 --> 01:22:34.323
And if we're at all lucky, it's
because enough people have come out

01:22:34.383 --> 01:22:39.385
and enough gay sons have died or whatever,
so parents are more, and younger people

01:22:39.425 --> 01:22:42.766
are more accepting of their gay friends
and things are a little better

01:22:42.806 --> 01:22:46.147
in the schools and all that,
and that's just an evolutionary thing.

01:22:47.928 --> 01:22:52.957
But you want to put people to sleep fast,
just say, the things that you're saying.

01:22:54.198 --> 01:22:58.199
I believe in conspiracies as much
as the next person, and I think

01:22:58.219 --> 01:23:02.541
that there are plenty
of those around against us particularly.

01:23:03.201 --> 01:23:09.344
My book is a history
of conspiracies, of hate against us.

01:23:10.324 --> 01:23:11.264
There's no shortage of them.

01:23:12.745 --> 01:23:16.007
What there is a shortage is of
people getting up and fighting back.

01:23:16.748 --> 01:23:18.409
And that's what you're talking about.

01:23:18.449 --> 01:23:21.370
And to this day, I do not know
why people don't fight back.

01:23:22.451 --> 01:23:25.773
And I go back to this Jews
walking into the gas chambers analogy.

01:23:26.113 --> 01:23:26.473
They did.

01:23:26.493 --> 01:23:29.075
I know I wouldn't have, I think.

01:23:32.857 --> 01:23:34.118
But an awful lot of them sure did.

01:23:34.598 --> 01:23:35.619
Is your new book a novel?

01:23:39.105 --> 01:23:39.966
I don't know what to call it.

01:23:40.446 --> 01:23:42.686
It was originally just called
The American People.

01:23:42.726 --> 01:23:44.367
Now I call it
The American People, a history.

01:23:46.167 --> 01:23:47.188
An awful lot of it is true.

01:23:47.688 --> 01:23:52.169
And a lot of it is fictionalized only
for legal reasons.

01:23:53.669 --> 01:23:56.150
And some of it is out and out fiction.

01:23:56.470 --> 01:23:57.450
But a lot of it is true.

01:23:57.711 --> 01:23:58.291
So I don't know.

01:23:59.491 --> 01:24:00.931
I would say over half of it is true.

01:24:01.171 --> 01:24:02.192
Are there characters?

01:24:02.752 --> 01:24:03.332
Oh, God, yes.

01:24:03.432 --> 01:24:03.872
Thousands.

01:24:05.013 --> 01:24:06.273
Cast of thousands.

01:24:07.717 --> 01:24:10.779
And is it truly, I mean, I've heard rumors
that it's thousands of pages.

01:24:10.799 --> 01:24:13.020
It's 3,000 at this point.

01:24:12.720 --> 01:24:13.480
3,000 pages.

01:24:13.900 --> 01:24:14.941
And they love it.

01:24:15.221 --> 01:24:16.062
My editor loves it.

01:24:16.342 --> 01:24:17.382
He doesn't want it shorter.

01:24:18.443 --> 01:24:19.423
So when is it coming?

01:24:20.004 --> 01:24:20.704
When it's finished.

01:24:21.745 --> 01:24:24.666
So Larry, do you think
that you are taken seriously as an artist?

01:24:24.846 --> 01:24:25.046
No.

01:24:25.627 --> 01:24:25.867
Why?

01:24:25.887 --> 01:24:27.588
Because I'm an activist.

01:24:28.668 --> 01:24:31.290
And so what
does that do in people's minds?

01:24:36.542 --> 01:24:41.566
I know I am not
a part of the literary gay lit mafia

01:24:41.586 --> 01:24:43.007
and I know that I'm not respected

01:24:43.047 --> 01:24:48.151
and I know I've been ostracized in
many ways and when there's a list

01:24:48.171 --> 01:24:53.034
of a hundred best gay this
and that put out

01:24:53.094 --> 01:24:55.476
by gay people, I'm never on it.

01:24:55.496 --> 01:24:57.417
And what do you think the reason is?

01:24:57.837 --> 01:25:01.640
I think it's because I'm an activist
and I'm known as this activist person

01:25:01.680 --> 01:25:06.421
and this is not a country
where where political involvement

01:25:06.641 --> 01:25:10.582
by creative artists
is—I don't like to call myself an artist,

01:25:10.602 --> 01:25:13.984
but you know what
I mean—by creative people is respected.

01:25:14.044 --> 01:25:20.827
It's not like I identify far more with,
you know, with German writers like Boll

01:25:20.867 --> 01:25:29.846
and Grass and and Sebold and
South American writers

01:25:32.608 --> 01:25:34.609
who write about it as a matter of course.

01:25:36.630 --> 01:25:40.073
Part of my fury at the gay writers
has been over just this,

01:25:42.374 --> 01:25:45.156
that in the height of AIDS, the people
like David Levitt and Ed White

01:25:45.176 --> 01:25:49.820
continue to write books that are nothing
about which are about nothing but fucking.

01:25:50.621 --> 01:25:51.942
And I'm sorry.

01:25:51.982 --> 01:25:54.244
I'm about more than my cock.

01:25:55.125 --> 01:25:56.586
And that makes a lot of people angry.

01:25:56.626 --> 01:25:57.988
That's a whole other conversation.

01:25:59.129 --> 01:26:01.451
But that's
why I'm ostracized, and it's fine.

01:26:01.491 --> 01:26:02.011
I can deal with it.

01:26:02.031 --> 01:26:02.912
I don't want to be their friend.

01:26:04.614 --> 01:26:04.914
Okay.

01:26:06.015 --> 01:26:08.878
I have a couple of other questions,
and then I think we're coming to an end.

01:26:09.478 --> 01:26:10.099
Oh, I like this.

01:26:10.865 --> 01:26:14.106
You're 68 years old,
and you've been living with

01:26:14.286 --> 01:26:16.647
HIV for about 20 years.

01:26:17.608 --> 01:26:19.789
You've survived a liver transplant.

01:26:20.349 --> 01:26:22.190
Basically,
you're having a normal lifespan.

01:26:23.310 --> 01:26:24.951
That must be shocking on some level.

01:26:26.812 --> 01:26:28.272
I never take anything for granted.

01:26:29.973 --> 01:26:32.394
Was there a moment when you realized,
wow, I'm having a full life?

01:26:34.535 --> 01:26:36.036
I don't let myself think that way.

01:26:39.028 --> 01:26:47.112
I remember coming out of the transplant,
out of ICU, and you never saw

01:26:47.232 --> 01:26:52.075
anybody bound into the hospital
with so much excitement as

01:26:52.195 --> 01:26:53.495
when they called me for my liver.

01:26:55.116 --> 01:26:59.839
And I was there with my lover David
and Roger, and it was Christmas time,

01:27:00.059 --> 01:27:02.880
and they had both gone back to New York,
and I was still in Pittsburgh,

01:27:03.320 --> 01:27:04.581
and I had taken a sleeping pill.

01:27:08.431 --> 01:27:12.172
The call came in at 11 o'clock at night,
get to the hospital as soon as possible.

01:27:17.935 --> 01:27:20.136
Getting a taxi in Pittsburgh is real hard.

01:27:21.016 --> 01:27:24.497
Somehow they got me there,
and I rushed into the hospital.

01:27:24.517 --> 01:27:25.898
I said, let's go, let's go, let's go.

01:27:26.738 --> 01:27:28.718
And the guy looked at me
and said, are you crazy?

01:27:28.838 --> 01:27:30.759
Do you know
what is about to happen to you?

01:27:31.559 --> 01:27:33.459
And I said,
I've been waiting for this a long time.

01:27:34.300 --> 01:27:40.221
And when I came out of the fog
I was in, you do a lot of thinking.

01:27:40.941 --> 01:27:41.421
Why me?

01:27:41.521 --> 01:27:42.721
How did I live through all of this?

01:27:42.781 --> 01:27:45.222
And everybody else said,
you think a lot about that.

01:27:45.922 --> 01:27:47.482
And it's like, why didn't everybody fight?

01:27:47.522 --> 01:27:47.902
I don't know.

01:27:47.962 --> 01:27:48.543
That's one of those.

01:27:49.923 --> 01:27:52.103
You can think about it
and you can make all kinds of promises.

01:27:52.563 --> 01:27:54.864
I don't happen to believe in
God, so you can't.

01:27:56.245 --> 01:27:57.165
deal with it on that level.

01:27:58.646 --> 01:28:01.108
And I haven't done since I was like 13.

01:28:05.211 --> 01:28:08.553
But you wonder, you think,
can't help but do that to you.

01:28:09.494 --> 01:28:10.694
I don't take anything for granted.

01:28:10.795 --> 01:28:17.714
The drugs for so many years
have been Uncomfortable, so you never...

01:28:17.734 --> 01:28:22.318
This is the last six months or so,
three months since I've been on

01:28:22.339 --> 01:28:24.701
these new drugs, this is the last time
I've actually felt human

01:28:24.981 --> 01:28:28.485
without having to deal with side effects
like diarrhea or constipation

01:28:29.866 --> 01:28:32.129
or whatever, and lack of energy.

01:28:32.970 --> 01:28:37.813
Dr. Fung, my surgeon, said,
in all seriousness,

01:28:38.815 --> 01:28:41.339
that I should easily have
another 20 years.

01:28:42.681 --> 01:28:46.386
I also have liver of a 45-year-old man,
and he said with all seriousness,

01:28:47.067 --> 01:28:49.691
you are as old
as your liver, so I'll take that too.

01:28:50.112 --> 01:28:54.077
But there's not a day
where I don't worry about

01:28:54.437 --> 01:28:57.560
something going wrong.

01:28:57.900 --> 01:29:01.123
We don't know what these medicines do for
you that we unleashed on the world.

01:29:01.724 --> 01:29:05.227
Every single one of them is found to have
so many different side effects

01:29:05.308 --> 01:29:08.190
and so many different kinds of people
that you can never take anything

01:29:08.230 --> 01:29:08.691
for granted.

01:29:08.731 --> 01:29:14.518
This wonderful regimen that
everybody dreamed up for me

01:29:14.958 --> 01:29:16.198
three weeks, three months ago.

01:29:17.739 --> 01:29:19.839
Another doctor has just come forth
and discovered something

01:29:19.859 --> 01:29:22.580
that may be bad for me particularly.

01:29:22.740 --> 01:29:28.982
So it's hard because all the,
you have to bet, you have to do

01:29:29.022 --> 01:29:31.142
different form of kinetics
between the HIV drug

01:29:31.302 --> 01:29:33.183
and the anti-rejection drug.

01:29:33.243 --> 01:29:35.923
And that takes a long time to balance
the amount

01:29:35.963 --> 01:29:38.244
of the program it's called in your blood.

01:29:38.996 --> 01:29:42.099
How much does your health care cost
per year, just out of curiosity?

01:29:42.279 --> 01:29:45.101
Well, that's a whole other issue.

01:29:48.644 --> 01:29:50.545
My liver transplant
costs Medicare $500,000.

01:29:52.026 --> 01:29:54.929
I sold the bill.

01:29:58.992 --> 01:30:00.793
I take one medicine a month.

01:30:02.335 --> 01:30:05.637
I get an infusion of something
called hepatitis B immune globulin.

01:30:05.677 --> 01:30:06.598
It costs $8,000 a month.

01:30:10.064 --> 01:30:21.709
And every time you go into the clinic
at Pittsburgh, there's always somebody

01:30:21.749 --> 01:30:28.632
who is either waiting there to waylay
a doctor to grab his leg and say, please,

01:30:28.713 --> 01:30:34.655
please, please, please take me
when they've been rejected for a liver,

01:30:35.055 --> 01:30:42.122
or somebody there who is The whole town
has sold everything, literally.

01:30:42.783 --> 01:30:45.765
The whole town has sold everything
so that they can be there.

01:30:47.967 --> 01:30:55.392
When I was there, I got accepted by
Medicare and that Belinda,

01:30:56.153 --> 01:31:00.556
black AIDS activist from Boston,
was turned down by Medicare.

01:31:01.857 --> 01:31:02.858
Same situation.

01:31:05.579 --> 01:31:09.702
And unfortunately, she raised the money,
but unfortunately,

01:31:10.703 --> 01:31:12.905
she didn't survive the transplant.

01:31:13.345 --> 01:31:17.809
So I have been
very lucky all along the line.

01:31:18.629 --> 01:31:19.790
I didn't need the AIDS drugs.

01:31:21.312 --> 01:31:24.714
When I needed a Hep B drug,
it suddenly was there

01:31:25.435 --> 01:31:26.516
in an experimental trial.

01:31:26.536 --> 01:31:28.958
When I needed a liver,
it was suddenly there, timing.

01:31:32.052 --> 01:31:34.993
I mean, I'm exceedingly grateful.

01:31:37.573 --> 01:31:44.075
That's why I work so hard on the book,
because that's my, if I've been spared,

01:31:44.115 --> 01:31:50.776
I think it's been
because I'm here to tell this story.

01:31:51.276 --> 01:31:57.278
I'm the only gay writer who was on
the front line, that's still alive,

01:31:57.298 --> 01:31:58.958
who was on
the front line from the very beginning.

01:32:01.058 --> 01:32:03.520
And I know where all the bodies
are buried,

01:32:04.000 --> 01:32:06.862
figuratively and metaphorically.

01:32:09.664 --> 01:32:11.465
No, actually and metaphorically, anyway.

01:32:12.546 --> 01:32:17.129
And that's what propels me every day.

01:32:17.329 --> 01:32:18.370
Seven days a week I write.

01:32:18.670 --> 01:32:19.450
That's why I'm up here.

01:32:21.392 --> 01:32:21.812
Okay.

01:32:22.913 --> 01:32:23.813
Just one last thing.

01:32:24.274 --> 01:32:28.716
At the beginning of the interview,
you were talking about discovering

01:32:28.736 --> 01:32:35.653
the level of, cruelty
and difference towards gay people.

01:32:35.893 --> 01:32:38.914
You said they didn't care that you went to
Yale, they didn't care

01:32:38.954 --> 01:32:40.635
if you had money, you were just a faggot.

01:32:41.696 --> 01:32:42.756
Do you think that that's still true?

01:32:42.776 --> 01:32:43.516
Oh, God, yes.

01:32:44.057 --> 01:32:45.978
That they don't care that you went to
Yale and that you have money?

01:32:45.998 --> 01:32:49.059
You don't think that makes a difference?

01:32:49.239 --> 01:32:50.320
Well,
it depends what you're talking about.

01:32:52.031 --> 01:32:55.012
Has that... Doesn't make any difference
to Gary Bauer, doesn't make

01:32:55.032 --> 01:32:56.332
any difference to George Bush.

01:32:57.193 --> 01:32:57.873
But in the years...

01:32:57.893 --> 01:32:58.913
Who went to Yale too.

01:32:58.933 --> 01:33:03.395
Okay, but in those years
since you started with GMHC to now,

01:33:03.895 --> 01:33:09.116
do those two factors, do they,
have they changed in terms of your ability

01:33:09.197 --> 01:33:10.577
to be seen by other people?

01:33:12.678 --> 01:33:13.278
Oh, of course.

01:33:13.538 --> 01:33:16.679
I mean, things have changed
and one should never deny

01:33:16.719 --> 01:33:17.399
that they haven't.

01:33:21.499 --> 01:33:25.461
You're either a cup-half-full person
or a cup-half-empty person,

01:33:25.541 --> 01:33:28.343
and I'm half-empty, I guess.

01:33:29.543 --> 01:33:35.206
I spent over 20 years trying to get
Yale to teach gay studies,

01:33:38.588 --> 01:33:42.130
offering them money all along the line,
and was turned down

01:33:42.510 --> 01:33:45.492
and was treated terribly, just terribly.

01:33:46.161 --> 01:33:47.182
Why did they turn you down?

01:33:47.382 --> 01:33:48.003
What is the real story?

01:33:48.083 --> 01:33:53.107
We don't want to, we don't believe in
isolating populations

01:33:53.188 --> 01:33:55.049
by themselves, separating them.

01:33:55.590 --> 01:33:58.492
And I said, well, you got women's studies,
and you got black studies,

01:33:58.533 --> 01:33:59.674
and you got Chinese studies.

01:34:01.956 --> 01:34:03.077
Why can't we have gay studies?

01:34:03.597 --> 01:34:05.719
And they have, well,
we don't think it's a discipline yet.

01:34:05.879 --> 01:34:08.121
We haven't earned
the right to be a discipline or whatever.

01:34:09.242 --> 01:34:10.183
Well, give us a chance.

01:34:10.263 --> 01:34:11.144
I mean, it was awful.

01:34:11.404 --> 01:34:15.446
And of course, Was this for gay
and lesbian studies

01:34:15.506 --> 01:34:16.627
or for gay male studies?

01:34:16.687 --> 01:34:20.689
No, that became a whole issue
that got way out

01:34:22.090 --> 01:34:24.051
in my will, which I had sent to them.

01:34:24.652 --> 01:34:27.834
I specified all kinds of courses
that could be given for men

01:34:28.034 --> 01:34:30.155
and for women, and I separated them.

01:34:30.696 --> 01:34:32.377
Lesbian literature, gay male literature.

01:34:33.217 --> 01:34:35.238
And for whatever reason, when the Times
ran the story,

01:34:35.278 --> 01:34:37.500
they only made the gay male literature.

01:34:37.540 --> 01:34:42.683
So lesbians got up in arms,
quite understandably, especially at Yale,

01:34:43.184 --> 01:34:44.444
that I was only interested in that.

01:34:44.504 --> 01:34:45.245
But that wasn't true.

01:34:46.386 --> 01:34:47.967
Anyway, they didn't want me.

01:34:48.147 --> 01:34:48.887
They didn't want the money.

01:34:48.947 --> 01:34:54.751
And of course, the only me they knew
was this angriest gay man in the world.

01:34:55.552 --> 01:34:59.074
So that did not go down well,
and they didn't treat me well.

01:34:59.114 --> 01:35:04.038
And it was only, again,
with a straight man got this on the,

01:35:04.098 --> 01:35:09.843
it was my good friend Calvin Trolling,
a straight man who had been on

01:35:09.863 --> 01:35:11.145
the Yale Corporation board.

01:35:12.426 --> 01:35:20.000
And he finally called up the president
and he said, you really should do this.

01:35:20.941 --> 01:35:22.502
And he's really a nice person.

01:35:23.263 --> 01:35:24.164
Why don't you meet him again?

01:35:24.444 --> 01:35:25.785
And then it went okay from there.

01:35:26.886 --> 01:35:31.010
And now there is, which I'm
exceedingly proud of,

01:35:31.550 --> 01:35:35.734
the Larry Kramer Initiative for Lesbian
and Gay Studies at Yale, endowed by

01:35:35.774 --> 01:35:41.640
my brother, a straight man,
which is going like gangbusters.

01:35:41.780 --> 01:35:43.763
What is it?

01:35:44.504 --> 01:35:51.833
It's a center for lesbian and gay studies
and they have faculty, they give awards,

01:35:52.113 --> 01:35:59.724
there are more speeches sponsored on
campus per year by us than

01:35:59.765 --> 01:36:01.726
by any other department,
everybody in the world.

01:36:01.846 --> 01:36:05.289
I'm going up next week to introduce
Gore Vidal, who's coming to speak,

01:36:05.730 --> 01:36:07.471
to LKI, we call it.

01:36:08.733 --> 01:36:11.495
Big deal, Kendall's now
on our board, Kendall Thomas.

01:36:16.491 --> 01:36:20.892
Other big people have been asked,
and Kendall is so excited about it.

01:36:21.153 --> 01:36:23.093
Anyway, after
all these years, they finally have it.

01:36:23.113 --> 01:36:25.294
I mean, mind you,
there's plenty wrong with it.

01:36:25.314 --> 01:36:26.174
We're not a department.

01:36:26.254 --> 01:36:29.815
We still don't have any tenured faculty,
all that sort of thing.

01:36:30.375 --> 01:36:32.176
But it is going like gangbusters.

01:36:32.316 --> 01:36:34.617
And LKI is in the papers every 10 minutes.

01:36:34.857 --> 01:36:38.458
And I'm, again, so very,
very, very proud of that.

01:36:39.684 --> 01:36:42.246
It took me a long time,
because I can remember, as I said,

01:36:42.266 --> 01:36:47.469
I'm the kid who tried to kill himself
freshman year because he was gay.

01:36:48.489 --> 01:36:49.930
And now there's this.

01:36:53.772 --> 01:36:58.595
And I started, as you probably know,
I started an award in honor

01:36:58.615 --> 01:37:02.838
of Sarah Pettit for a lesbian,
some kind of lesbian scholarship.

01:37:03.138 --> 01:37:05.379
And that's going to happen there
as part of this.

01:37:07.193 --> 01:37:10.414
And it's become the center,
an incredible man called Jonathan Katz,

01:37:10.534 --> 01:37:14.294
Jonathan David Katz, who runs it.

01:37:14.874 --> 01:37:15.875
The art historian?

01:37:15.915 --> 01:37:16.135
Yes.

01:37:16.155 --> 01:37:16.495
Yeah, okay.

01:37:17.135 --> 01:37:17.975
And is this new to you?

01:37:18.715 --> 01:37:20.735
Yeah, I'm not,
I'm vaguely aware of it, you know.

01:37:20.815 --> 01:37:21.296
Oh, he's come.

01:37:21.876 --> 01:37:22.316
I didn't know.

01:37:22.336 --> 01:37:28.277
He took, yes, he's gotten Robert Gober
to go up there and talk, and he's doing

01:37:28.317 --> 01:37:33.218
a big exhibition on, I forgot,
Lichtenstein, not Lichtenstein,

01:37:33.258 --> 01:37:39.415
Rauschenberg,
and He's totally, totally amazing.

01:37:45.099 --> 01:37:46.840
I can't remember the name
of that woman, I'll think about it.

01:37:46.900 --> 01:37:50.203
Anyway, a lot of people are, have been.

01:37:50.383 --> 01:37:56.127
So basically, in terms of
within your class, your position

01:37:56.188 --> 01:37:58.349
as a gay man has really been transformed.

01:37:59.110 --> 01:38:00.791
That now you're participating in
your class

01:38:00.851 --> 01:38:02.352
more fully than you were able to.

01:38:02.452 --> 01:38:03.133
What's my class?

01:38:03.840 --> 01:38:09.481
Yale, or the, what you said at
the beginning, that regardless of

01:38:09.721 --> 01:38:14.802
how many resources you had,
as a gay man you were not seen.

01:38:14.962 --> 01:38:16.403
And now they're saying, okay.

01:38:17.203 --> 01:38:17.663
So there is a...

01:38:17.743 --> 01:38:19.983
Well, I mean, I don't consider
that Yale has welcomed me

01:38:20.023 --> 01:38:20.963
into this open arms.

01:38:21.023 --> 01:38:25.884
I consider that, you know,
I'm still having fights

01:38:25.904 --> 01:38:26.985
with everybody about things.

01:38:27.965 --> 01:38:28.565
And we're there...

01:38:30.362 --> 01:38:33.665
Yes, they've let us in the door,
but you never should ever feel like you're

01:38:33.705 --> 01:38:35.206
welcome forever at the dinner table.

01:38:35.586 --> 01:38:36.387
And I feel that way.

01:38:37.647 --> 01:38:42.211
But you talked earlier
about people needing people's approval.

01:38:43.412 --> 01:38:47.515
And that should never be
a motivating factor.

01:38:48.736 --> 01:38:52.639
I threatened to resign
only last week over a fight.

01:38:53.399 --> 01:38:54.840
So I am what I am.

01:38:55.301 --> 01:38:56.281
And my brother screamed.

01:38:57.338 --> 01:38:57.979
You what?

01:38:58.900 --> 01:38:59.801
That's your legacy.

01:38:59.821 --> 01:39:01.263
I paid for this legacy.

01:39:01.303 --> 01:39:01.944
What was the fight?

01:39:02.204 --> 01:39:05.829
Was it anything important,
or were you just threatening?

01:39:06.089 --> 01:39:07.050
No, it's a principle.

01:39:08.712 --> 01:39:11.596
The provost did something behind my back,
and I'm chair of a committee,

01:39:11.616 --> 01:39:13.438
and he shouldn't have done it,
and I got very mad.

01:39:14.192 --> 01:39:16.113
Larry, I forgot something
I just wanted to ask you, and this is

01:39:16.133 --> 01:39:19.636
just a personal question,
but I remember Vito Russo's funeral.

01:39:19.656 --> 01:39:21.037
Whose? Vito.

01:39:21.077 --> 01:39:21.277
Yeah.

01:39:21.958 --> 01:39:25.641
You made this speech
where you said, we killed Vito.

01:39:26.121 --> 01:39:27.282
Don't you know that?

01:39:27.422 --> 01:39:28.163
Can't you see that?

01:39:28.183 --> 01:39:28.503
Mm-hmm.

01:39:29.944 --> 01:39:36.289
And I remember feeling
as I was sitting there that I was not

01:39:36.309 --> 01:39:37.990
the appropriate target of that speech.

01:39:38.010 --> 01:39:40.312
That was just a personal reaction
that I had sitting there.

01:39:40.332 --> 01:39:44.135
And I'm wondering if you have had
any hindsight onto that kind

01:39:44.175 --> 01:39:46.697
of rhetoric or that sort of approach.

01:39:52.921 --> 01:39:53.902
You're too sensitive.

01:39:57.245 --> 01:40:00.168
I have tried in
my time many kinds of rhetoric.

01:40:00.348 --> 01:40:04.692
You read reports from the Holocaust
and there are many attempts

01:40:04.772 --> 01:40:07.134
at different tactics and voices.

01:40:08.456 --> 01:40:10.377
Sometimes you need one,
sometimes you need another.

01:40:10.958 --> 01:40:11.619
You keep looking.

01:40:11.819 --> 01:40:13.120
You make it up as you go along.

01:40:14.021 --> 01:40:21.147
When Vito died, everybody was dying
and there still weren't that many people

01:40:21.208 --> 01:40:22.928
out there fighting so we did kill veto

01:40:23.929 --> 01:40:27.691
and yes you were a tactic
i mean yes you were a target everybody was

01:40:27.751 --> 01:40:33.334
a target as many people were in that room
didn't equal the membership of act up

01:40:34.774 --> 01:40:35.135
so i

01:40:36.315 --> 01:40:43.746
i mean i yell at
gay people still We're all targets.

01:40:44.066 --> 01:40:45.046
I still don't do enough.

01:40:45.527 --> 01:40:52.068
I get angry at myself because, because,
because, because, because I can't do

01:40:52.128 --> 01:40:53.108
all the things I want to do.

01:40:54.868 --> 01:40:57.609
Crying at lunch
with Anna all very last week

01:40:57.789 --> 01:41:02.050
because I would still like to work with
GMHC.

01:41:02.630 --> 01:41:04.470
And she's made
it possible for me to do that.

01:41:05.930 --> 01:41:06.690
And I'm going to do it.

01:41:09.898 --> 01:41:14.981
And that moves me very much,
that the people are all new,

01:41:15.761 --> 01:41:16.982
changed, and they want me back.

01:41:18.502 --> 01:41:22.684
But I also know that in doing that,
the novel gets a little short shrifted.

01:41:22.804 --> 01:41:24.785
The book, I shouldn't call it
a novel, gets short shrifted.

01:41:25.526 --> 01:41:26.386
And that's hard.

01:41:27.747 --> 01:41:30.748
I would much rather work in New York
than here, because when David's not here,

01:41:30.788 --> 01:41:31.769
I get a little stir crazy.

01:41:33.430 --> 01:41:36.671
And so I think, oh,
why can't I go work in New York?

01:41:39.113 --> 01:41:42.095
I got a nice apartment and it's quiet
and blah, blah, blah, but the minute

01:41:42.115 --> 01:41:43.696
you go back to New York,
you get sucked into

01:41:43.736 --> 01:41:46.218
by the electricity of New York.

01:41:46.258 --> 01:41:48.879
You want to see Tony, my friend
Tony Kushner, or you want to go see

01:41:50.160 --> 01:41:53.422
the play or the movies or whatever,
things that

01:41:53.442 --> 01:41:55.043
you somehow manage to keep at bay.

01:41:56.164 --> 01:41:57.366
But it happens here, too.

01:41:57.386 --> 01:42:01.490
I got to go to Yale and introduce
Gore Vidal next week, and then I agreed.

01:42:01.750 --> 01:42:05.355
One of my doctor's nurses is one
of the heads of the American Association

01:42:05.395 --> 01:42:07.217
of Nurses with AIDS,
and would I please come

01:42:07.237 --> 01:42:08.298
and address their convention?

01:42:08.318 --> 01:42:12.302
I have 500 nurses
next week, so I'm going to do that.

01:42:12.382 --> 01:42:14.765
And then Judy Falloon says,
would you please come to

01:42:14.805 --> 01:42:15.786
this convention at the NIH?

01:42:15.806 --> 01:42:19.972
Because all these scientists
have never seen a person like you

01:42:19.992 --> 01:42:22.034
I mean all they do is research
they've never seen a person

01:42:22.074 --> 01:42:24.318
who's been through what you have
and would you talk to them about that

01:42:24.338 --> 01:42:29.344
and of course great that's what I'm
that's my usefulness and all of that

01:42:29.364 --> 01:42:36.025
so I have to find
a way of of dealing with all of this.

01:42:38.166 --> 01:42:41.427
Tony and I talk about this a lot
because he would much rather be

01:42:41.467 --> 01:42:44.048
an activist, he says.

01:42:44.408 --> 01:42:51.952
And there's an essay I wrote at the end
of the book, Women and Love, of my stuff,

01:42:53.553 --> 01:42:59.575
where he said, well,
you realize that it's not an either or.

01:42:59.615 --> 01:43:01.316
You've got to
somehow find a way of doing both.

01:43:03.226 --> 01:43:07.329
And fortunately,
you have 20 more years to do it.

01:43:07.369 --> 01:43:08.830
From your mouth to somebody's ears.

01:43:08.890 --> 01:43:09.711
Thank you, Larry.

01:43:09.911 --> 01:43:10.872
Thank you very much.

01:43:10.932 --> 01:43:14.795
Thank you.

01:43:16.396 --> 01:43:17.097
How long did we go?

01:43:17.117 --> 01:43:18.698
We did...

